#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""glaeda-disk: say where the disk went, and reclaim what is safe to rebuild.

    glaeda-disk                     report: biggest items, verdicts, reclaimable total
    glaeda-disk --apply             delete every reclaimable item, largest first
    glaeda-disk --pressure --apply  act only under disk pressure, stop at the free target
    glaeda-disk --idle --apply      while no job runs, delete what sat idle past its family's age
    glaeda-disk --pressure --idle --apply  pressure first; with no pressure, an hourly idle sweep
    glaeda-disk --low 15% --target 25%  thresholds as GiB free or as a share of each filesystem
    glaeda-disk --pressure --apply waits while a GitHub Actions job runs, unless below --emergency
    glaeda-disk --refresh           re-measure every size now instead of using the snapshot
    glaeda-disk --dedupe            store identical Xcode package/module files once (APFS clones)

Parallel sessions each keep a full DerivedData, and its SourcePackages artifacts and module
caches are byte-identical across them. --dedupe replaces each duplicate with a copy-on-write
clone of one canonical file, keeping the duplicate's mode and timestamps. Builds see the same
bytes; the disk stores them once. du still counts clones in full; df shows the real saving.

macOS keeps no directory sizes (Spotlight has none for folders), so measuring
means walking every file, which is disk-latency bound. Sizes are therefore kept
in a snapshot: a report prints instantly from it, re-judges every verdict live
(processes, idle time, git), sizes only new items, and re-measures in the
background once the snapshot is older than --max-age minutes.

Only families whose contents are reproducible build output or scratch space are
ever deleted. Everything else is reported so the space is at least visible.
An item is never deleted while a process has its cwd inside it, a process command
line names it, it holds a git checkout, or it changed within the idle window.
A cache is recognized by its content, never its name (cache_layout: a CACHEDIR.TAG,
npm's cacache, a hex-sharded content store like Go's build cache, bun's install cache,
a rustup toolchain, Go's module cache, a Chromium disk cache, a Bazel output root).
In the cache families (not tmp or scratch, whose checkouts are judged in full) such a
tree is "sealed": the git search checks its top two levels, where a person's clone would
sit, and does not walk the millions of blobs below. Any other tree is searched in full,
and one too large to finish, unreadable, or holding another filesystem stays unchecked (kept).
The home-caches family finds caches anywhere in the home directory by the layouts that name
the tool refilling them (DISCOVERABLE: ~/.npm/_cacache, ~/.bun/install/cache, a cargo-tagged
~/.cargo/registry, ~/go/pkg/mod, a rustup toolchain nothing pins), never a Python environment
(uv and direnv tag those too). Least recently used goes first, counting file access where the
filesystem records it; a cache in use is kept by process evidence. Secrets, keychains,
checkouts, other filesystems, the Chromium trees and the macOS privacy-protected folders are
never searched (HOME_NEVER).
Bazel's macOS output root (/private/var/tmp/_bazel_$USER) is its own family.
Every deletion is appended to a JSONL receipt.

The idle sweep (--idle) reclaims whatever the free space. A busy host sweeps too: deletes skip only what a
job uses (open, named by a command, a root's current seed or checkout), not the whole pass. The old hold
(no sweep while a GitHub Actions job, build, dev-build worker job, reservation or held host lock) and the
pressure pass's defer-while-CI-runs come back with "defer_during_ci": true in ~/.config/glaeda/disk-policy.json.
Its windows follow reuse (IDLE_SWEEP_HOURS and friends). cmux CI's hot tier ages in host CI jobs since its
last use, not hours (item_due: pressure_jobs, min_jobs, sweep_jobs, soft_idle_hours in disk-policy.json). It also
reports Xcode copies no pin names and simulator runtimes, and deletes a runtime only when no device
uses it, it is not its platform's newest, it went unused for RUNTIME_IDLE_DAYS and no simulator is
booted (simctl runtime delete restarts CoreSimulatorService). Xcode copies are never deleted.

On an owned cmux mini, cmux CI's warm state under /Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/ci is a local hot
tier, evicted LRU under pressure: parked pull request builds unused for 30 minutes, seeds other than each
root's newest one under pressure (two during idle sweeps), and a root's kept build once the root has been idle two days. On the
trusted seeder, a seed its serve log shows streamed whole to the seed archive (cmux-lawrence's SSD) is
reclaimable at once instead of after the idle window: the PR minis restore it from there over the LAN.
SwiftPM scratch (spm-scratch/<fingerprint>) that no job holds by its lock ages like any cache, and goes
whatever its age once free space is below the emergency floor. The Chromium / cmux-browser trees and
their caches are never deleted (NEVER_DELETE).

Free space is judged per filesystem. On Linux /tmp is often its own tmpfs, so pressure there
reclaims only /tmp items, and pressure on the home filesystem never deletes from /tmp.
Thresholds scale with the disk: "15%:40-150" is 15% of it, at least 40 and at most 150 GiB,
so the same default fits a 256 GB laptop, a Mac mini and a multi-terabyte Mac Studio.

A tmpfs holds its files in RAM and swap, so its free space says nothing about pressure: a
16 GiB /tmp at 80% had filled swap while still "2.3G free". A tmpfs is judged by
--tmpfs-low/--tmpfs-target instead (default: pressure once a quarter of it is in use), and
its items count from 1 MiB.

The temp dir also counts top-level files (leaked extracted libraries, logs), and a scratch
checkout there is reclaimable when deleting it loses nothing
git holds: clean, no stash, no other worktrees, and every commit it points at (refs, HEAD,
reflogs) on a remote-tracking ref or confirmed by a remote server (`fetch --negotiate-only`:
no objects move, no refs change; unreachable means keep). Every submodule repository it would
take along, in its git dir or embedded in its tree, must pass the same test; pinned submodule
commits fetched by id are what the server confirms. A linked worktree must be unlocked, with
HEAD on a branch, tag or remote ref of its repository, which survives, and no commit only its
own reflog holds. Ignored files go with it: in a temp-dir checkout they are
build output or copies. Nested or unverifiable checkouts stay protected.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import bisect
import contextlib
import fcntl
import json
import os
import platform
import pwd
import math
import re
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
from dataclasses import asdict, dataclass, field, replace
from pathlib import Path

HOME = Path.home()
SNAPSHOT = HOME / ".cache/glaeda-disk/sizes.json"
DEDUPE_STATE = HOME / ".cache/glaeda-disk/dedupe.json"
MEASURE_LOCK = HOME / ".cache/glaeda-disk/measure.lock"
ACCOUNTING = HOME / ".cache/glaeda-disk/accounting.json"
HEALTH_SUMMARY = HOME / ".local/state/glaeda/disk/summary.json"
EVICT_LOCK = HOME / ".cache/glaeda-disk/evict.lock"  # one --apply at a time (the runner hooks and the LaunchAgent)
# Items a nested_git family kept for a git loss, so a dead session with unpushed work is not
# re-walked every run. Only keep verdicts are cached: a stale entry can delay a deletion, never cause one.
NESTED_KEEP = HOME / ".cache/glaeda-disk/nested-keep.json"
NESTED_KEEP_S = 24 * 3600
# Session scratch holds whole source copies nested deeper than git_state's search; finding every
# checkout in one took ~48 s on a loaded Air Blue (glaeda#1293). Paid only by idle items.
NESTED_DEPTH, NESTED_BUDGET = 40, 5_000_000
DEDUPE_SUBDIRS = ("SourcePackages", "ModuleCache.noindex", "SDKExplicitPrecompiledModules")
DEDUPE_MIN_BYTES = 1024 * 1024
GIB = 1024**3
DARWIN = platform.system() == "Darwin"


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Family:
    id: str
    root: Path
    reclaimable: bool
    rebuild: str
    depth: int = 1  # candidates are directories this many levels below root
    skip: tuple[str, ...] = ()
    # when set, candidates are directories with this name found up to `depth` levels below
    # root (the first match on a path wins), instead of every directory exactly `depth` down
    match: str = ""
    marker: str = ""  # with match: the candidate must also contain this file
    files: bool = False  # top-level regular files are candidates too
    min_bytes: int | None = None  # size floor for this family instead of --min-mib
    git_disposable: bool = False  # a checkout that loses nothing when deleted is reclaimable
    nested_git: bool = False  # judge every checkout inside an item, not only one rooted at it
    owner: str = ""  # who writes it, so a cache nobody owns any more is visible
    owner_job: str = ""  # launchd service of that owner (e.g. system/ai.x.worker); unloaded = retired
    rebuild_minutes: float = 10.0  # rough cost of losing it; cheaper items go first under pressure
    skip_prefixes: tuple[str, ...] = ()  # candidates whose name starts with one of these are skipped
    # size every missing candidate with one `du -d1` walk of root instead of one du per candidate:
    # a macOS $TMPDIR holds tens of thousands of small leaked directories (25,730 on Air Blue)
    bulk_sizes: bool = False
    build_output: bool = False  # DerivedData-like: SwiftPM clones inside do not make it a checkout
    include: tuple[str, ...] = ()  # when set, only candidates whose name starts with one of these
    min_idle_hours: float = 0.0  # an item must also be idle this long, whatever the idle window
    max_idle_hours: float | None = None  # a hot-tier item goes once idle this long, whatever the idle window
    # candidates are the sealed caches discover_caches finds below root, not root's children
    discover: bool = False
    # candidates are the build outputs inside the checkouts under root (checkout_builds), not root's children
    checkout_builds: bool = False
    retired_only: bool = False  # reclaim only with fresh evidence the declared service is unloaded


def idle_window(fam: Family, idle_hours: float) -> float:
    window = max(idle_hours, fam.min_idle_hours)
    return window if fam.max_idle_hours is None else min(window, fam.max_idle_hours)


# Never deleted by any family, at any pressure (Leo, 2026-09-27): the Chromium / cmux-browser dev trees
# and their caches. A lost tree is a ~17 min LAN re-seed at best and a multi-hour Chromium build at worst,
# and a campaign may hold work nowhere else. A candidate at, inside or above one of these paths, or whose
# name matches NEVER_DELETE_NAMES, is dropped before it is judged.
NEVER_DELETE = ("cmux-browser-fleet", "Library/Caches/cmux-release-support", "Library/Caches/siso")  # under HOME
NEVER_DELETE_NAMES = re.compile(r"chromium|cmux-browser|siso|depot_tools|cmux-release-support|vpython|cipd", re.I)


def never_delete(path: Path) -> bool:
    if NEVER_DELETE_NAMES.search(path.name):
        return True
    p = os.path.realpath(path)
    for keep in NEVER_DELETE:
        k = os.path.realpath(HOME / keep)
        if p == k or p.startswith(k + "/") or k.startswith(p + "/"):
            return True
    return False


# cmux CI's warm state on an owned mini (cmux scripts/ci/owned_build_state.py, seed_derived_data.py).
# Root 1 keeps it in FLEET_CI, root k in FLEET_CI/cmux-ci-<k>. This is the local hot tier: every item is
# a copy of something a job can fetch or rebuild (seed bucket, the PR's next compile), so it is evicted
# LRU under pressure, keeping what the next job most likely starts from.
FLEET_CI = Path("/Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/ci")
SEED_SOURCE = "seed-source.json"
SEED_KEEP = 2  # seed_derived_data.LOCAL_KEEP_LOW_DISK: newest seeds of the current prefix each root keeps
KEPT_BUILD_IDLE_HOURS = 48.0
PARKED_IDLE_HOURS = float("inf")  # parked builds age by host-job reuse distance, not wall clock


def fleet_stores(ci: Path | None = None) -> list[Path]:
    ci = FLEET_CI if ci is None else ci
    if not ci.is_dir():
        return []
    return [ci, *sorted(p for p in ci.glob("cmux-ci-*")
                        if p.name[len("cmux-ci-"):].isdigit() and p.is_dir() and not p.is_symlink())]


FLEET_FAMILY_IDS = ("cmux-pr-builds", "cmux-seeds", "cmux-kept-build", "ci-derived-data")


def fleet_families(ci: Path | None = None) -> list[Family]:
    fams = []
    for store in fleet_stores(ci):
        # A parked pull request build (owned_build_state.py, usable by reuse distance) has no disk floor of
        # its own: two per root grew a mini's use by ~38 GiB within hours of landing (cmux14, 2026-09-27).
        # Job activity, not wall-clock age, decides when a parked build is stale.
        fams.append(Family("cmux-pr-builds", store / "pr-builds", True,
                           "the pull request's next push compiles from main's kept build",
                           include=("pr-",), build_output=True, max_idle_hours=PARKED_IDLE_HOURS,
                           owner="cmux owned_build_state.py (park)", rebuild_minutes=6))
        # Seeds: the root's newest SEED_KEEP of its current prefix stay (seed_verdict); older and other
        # fingerprints' seeds go LRU. A seed comes back from the LAN seeder or the bucket in 25 to 280 s.
        # On the trusted seeder, one the seed archive already holds goes without waiting (archived_seeds).
        fams.append(Family("cmux-seeds", store / "seeds", True,
                           "glaeda-seed-prefetch or the next job fetches it again", build_output=True,
                           owner="cmux seed_derived_data.py (CMUX_SEED_LOCAL_CACHE)", rebuild_minutes=4))
        # A root's kept build (and a discard a killed job left) goes only when the root is dead.
        fams.append(Family("cmux-kept-build", store, True, "the next job on this root starts from a seed",
                           include=("derived-data", ".derived-data.discard-"), build_output=True,
                           min_idle_hours=KEPT_BUILD_IDLE_HOURS,
                           owner="cmux owned_build_state.py (keep)", rebuild_minutes=10))
    if fams:  # root 1's store is the mini's, and the only one that holds SwiftPM scratch (cmux mini_store)
        fams.append(spm_scratch_family(fleet_stores(ci)[0]))
    return fams


# cmux CI's SwiftPM scratch (cmux scripts/ci/owned_spm_scratch.py): swift-package-tests links each package's .build
# to STORE/spm-scratch/<fingerprint>/<package> so a job rebuilds only what its change touched. A job holds
# <fingerprint>.lock shared (a holder process) until the runner ends it; `link` touches the lock, so its time is the
# directory's last use. Its owner bounds it only at a link (24 GiB LRU), so nothing evicted it under pressure:
# cmux9s held 31 GiB of it, none held by a job, while glaeda-disk --pressure found 0.0 GiB reclaimable and every
# runner's job-started hook refused at 21.9 GiB free under its 30 GiB floor (2026-09-30). A directory goes only
# under owned_spm_scratch's own protocol (remove_spm_scratch); its .trash-* leftovers always go.
SPM_SCRATCH = "cmux-spm-scratch"
SPM_TRASH = ".trash-"  # owned_spm_scratch.TRASH: a directory renamed aside for deletion, never reused
SPM_REBUILD_MINUTES = 3.0  # one swift-package-tests job rebuilds the packages its change selects


def spm_scratch_family(store: Path) -> Family:
    return Family(SPM_SCRATCH, store / "spm-scratch", True,
                  "the next swift-package-tests job rebuilds the packages it selects", build_output=True,
                  owner="cmux owned_spm_scratch.py", rebuild_minutes=SPM_REBUILD_MINUTES)


def spm_lock(path: Path) -> Path:
    return path.with_name(path.name + ".lock")


def spm_scratch_held(path: Path) -> bool:
    """Whether a job holds this scratch directory: owned_spm_scratch's holder keeps a shared flock on
    <fingerprint>.lock for the whole job. Probed exclusive and released at once, as owned_spm_scratch's own drop()
    does: its link takes the lock blocking, so it waits out the probe; its prune skips the directory for that moment.
    No lock file means no job ever linked it. An unreadable lock counts as held."""
    if path.name.startswith(SPM_TRASH):
        return False
    try:
        fd = os.open(spm_lock(path), os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return False
    except OSError:
        return True
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except OSError:
        return True
    finally:
        os.close(fd)  # closing drops the probe's lock
    return False


def remove_spm_scratch(path: Path) -> bool:
    """owned_spm_scratch.drop(): under an exclusive lock on <fingerprint>.lock, rename the directory aside to
    .trash-<name>-<pid> (which owned_spm_scratch also sweeps, should this delete be killed) and drop its .size, then
    delete it outside the lock. False, touching nothing, while a job holds it."""
    if path.is_symlink():
        raise OSError("refusing symlink")
    if os.path.ismount(path) or path.lstat().st_dev != path.parent.stat().st_dev:
        raise OSError("refusing a mount point")
    if path.name.startswith(SPM_TRASH):
        shutil.rmtree(path, onerror=_chmod_retry)
        return True
    with open(spm_lock(path), "a") as handle:  # created as owned_spm_scratch creates it
        try:
            fcntl.flock(handle, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except BlockingIOError:
            return False
        aside = path.with_name(f"{SPM_TRASH}{path.name}-{os.getpid()}")
        os.rename(path, aside)
        path.with_name(path.name + ".size").unlink(missing_ok=True)
    shutil.rmtree(aside, onerror=_chmod_retry)
    return True


# Filesystems whose free space is below the emergency floor (emergency_bytes) in this pressure run or report. There
# the SwiftPM scratch no job holds goes whatever its age: it rebuilds in minutes, and the runner floor is near.
EMERGENCY_DEVS: frozenset[int] = frozenset()


def emergency_devs(fss: "dict[int, Fs]", emergency: str, floor_gib: float = 0.0) -> frozenset[int]:
    return frozenset(fs.dev for fs in fss.values() if fs.free < emergency_bytes(fs, emergency, floor_gib))


# The trusted seeder's serve log (glaeda-seed-serve): the seed archive on cmux-lawrence's SSD fills itself from
# it every 5 minutes (glaeda-seed-archive), asking seed-list-v1 and then one seed-v1 per seed it lacks.
SEED_SERVE_LOG = HOME / ".local/state/glaeda/seed-serve/serve.jsonl"


def archived_seeds(log: Path | None = None) -> frozenset[str]:
    """Seed keys the seed archive holds, as far as this seeder's serve log (and the rotated one) can show.

    Only the archive's filler asks seed-list-v1, so a client address that lists is the archive. A clean
    `served` alone is not enough: the filler still checks and renames the file afterwards and drops it on
    failure. Each run lists first, then asks for every seed it lacks, so two later lists from that client
    with no new request for the key mean the archive saw the seed as present. The PR minis restore it from
    there over the LAN (63 to 184 s against about 15 minutes cold), so it need not wait out the idle window.
    A client that lied about being the archive can only make a seed go early, and the next job refetches it.
    An unreadable or rotated-away log proves nothing, so the seed keeps its usual window.
    """
    log = SEED_SERVE_LOG if log is None else log
    records: list[dict] = []
    for path in (log.with_name(log.name + ".1"), log):
        try:
            text = path.read_text(errors="replace")
        except OSError:
            continue
        for line in text.splitlines():
            with contextlib.suppress(ValueError, RecursionError):
                record = json.loads(line)
                if isinstance(record, dict) and isinstance(record.get("client"), str) \
                        and record.get("role") == "seed":
                    records.append(record)
    lists: dict[str, int] = {}  # client -> lists seen after the record being read (walking backwards)
    decided: set[str] = set()
    held: set[str] = set()
    for r in reversed(records):
        client, outcome, key = r["client"], r.get("outcome"), r.get("key")
        if outcome == "list":
            lists[client] = lists.get(client, 0) + 1
        elif isinstance(key, str) and key not in decided and outcome in ("served", "failed"):
            decided.add(key)  # only a key's newest request counts
            if outcome == "served" and r.get("status") == 0 and lists.get(client, 0) >= 2:
                held.add(key)
    return frozenset(held)


def item_window(fam: Family, path: Path, idle_hours: float, archived: frozenset[str] = frozenset()) -> float:
    """The idle window for one candidate: a seed the archive already holds may go at once (the newest of each
    root still stay, by keep_verdict), and SwiftPM scratch left in the trash or under the emergency floor
    (EMERGENCY_DEVS) may go at once (its lock keeps what a job holds). Everything else waits out its family's
    window."""
    if fam.id == "cmux-seeds" and path.name in archived:
        return 0.0
    if fam.id == SPM_SCRATCH:
        if path.name.startswith(SPM_TRASH):
            return 0.0
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            if fam.root.stat().st_dev in EMERGENCY_DEVS:
                return 0.0
    return idle_window(fam, idle_hours)


# The idle sweep (--idle): windows from how often each family is read again, not from free space.
# cmux restores parked PR builds by host-job reuse distance, so a quiet fleet does not age them out. A job
# touches the seed it starts from, so a seed's mtime is its last use.
# A busy root lands about four new ~9 GiB seeds an hour; one no job picked for a day (beyond the root's
# newest SEED_KEEP, which stay) sits far behind every new base, and a fetch brings it back in 25 to 280 s.
# A day, not less: cmux keeps up to 48 per root on a roomy disk so a PR on an older base starts close. DerivedData, caches and scratch get KEPT_BUILD_IDLE_HOURS, the
# window a root's own kept build already has: two days covers a quiet day or a weekend's first job.
IDLE_SWEEP_HOURS = KEPT_BUILD_IDLE_HOURS
IDLE_PR_BUILD_HOURS = float("inf")
IDLE_SEED_HOURS = 24.0
IDLE_SWEEP_EVERY_S = 3600  # the 15-minute agent sweeps at most hourly: an apply re-measures every candidate
IDLE_STAMP = HOME / ".cache/glaeda-disk/idle-sweep.last"
FLEET_DIR = FLEET_CI.parent
BUILD_PROCESS = re.compile(r"(^|/)(xcodebuild|SWBBuildService|XCBBuildService)(\s|$)")


def idle_families(fams: list[Family]) -> list[Family]:
    """The families as the idle sweep judges them: the hot tier gets its reuse windows."""
    windows = {"cmux-pr-builds": IDLE_PR_BUILD_HOURS, "cmux-seeds": IDLE_SEED_HOURS}
    return [replace(f, max_idle_hours=windows[f.id]) if f.id in windows else f for f in fams]


# Activity, not wall clock (Leo, 2026-09-29): a cache idle for 5 h because the whole fleet was idle is not
# stale, nothing had a chance to use it. So the hot tier (FLEET_FAMILY_IDS) ages in host CI jobs: how many
# jobs started on this host (the runner hook's job log) since the item was last used. Measured on 13 minis,
# 2026-09-26..29, 11,393 job starts: a job of the same PR (or push ref) came back to the same host after
# p50 6, p75 23, p90 76, p95 189, p99 431 other jobs (0.4 / 1.4 / 5.2 / 15.5 / 46 h), so wall clock says
# little (p90 5 h, p99 46 h) while the job count separates reuse from cruft. Under pressure an item goes
# once pressure_jobs (~p90) jobs passed it, or once it is past its family's window with at least min_jobs
# (~p50) jobs since. The idle sweep (no pressure) takes one past sweep_jobs (~p95), or one idle
# soft_idle_hours with pressure_jobs since: the soft 24 h rule, never an age cap on a quiet fleet. A host
# without a job log keeps the wall-clock windows. Every threshold is a key of DISK_POLICY.
DISK_POLICY = HOME / ".config/glaeda/disk-policy.json"
POLICY_DEFAULTS: dict[str, Any] = {
    "low": "25%:60-200",
    "target": "35%:100-350",
    "emergency": "10%:30-60",
    "low_margin_gib": 4.0,
    "target_margin_gib": 20.0,
    "pressure_idle_hours": 0.5,
    "pressure_jobs": 80,
    "min_jobs": 6,
    "sweep_jobs": 190,
    "soft_idle_hours": 24.0,
    # A busy host cleans too: deletes skip only what a job uses (open, named by a command, its root's
    # current seed or checkout), never the whole pass. true restores the old defer-while-CI-runs rule.
    "defer_during_ci": False,
}
ACTIVITY_MODE: str | None = None  # "pressure" or "sweep" while main acts; None keeps the wall-clock rule


def disk_policy(path: Path | None = None) -> dict[str, Any]:
    """POLICY_DEFAULTS overlaid with DISK_POLICY; an unreadable file or a wrongly typed key keeps the default."""
    policy = dict(POLICY_DEFAULTS)
    try:
        doc = json.loads((DISK_POLICY if path is None else path).read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return policy
    if isinstance(doc, dict):
        for key, default in POLICY_DEFAULTS.items():
            value = doc.get(key)
            if isinstance(default, bool):
                if isinstance(value, bool):
                    policy[key] = value
            elif isinstance(default, str):
                if isinstance(value, str) and value:
                    policy[key] = value
            elif isinstance(value, (int, float)) and not isinstance(value, bool) and value >= 0:
                if math.isfinite(value):
                    policy[key] = type(default)(value)
    return policy


POLICY = disk_policy()
_JOB_STARTS: list[float] | None = None
_JOB_STARTS_READ = False


def job_starts(log: Path | None = None) -> list[float] | None:
    """Sorted start times of this host's CI jobs (glaeda-cmux-jobs.jsonl "started" lines), or None without a log."""
    global _JOB_STARTS, _JOB_STARTS_READ
    if log is None and _JOB_STARTS_READ:
        return _JOB_STARTS
    path = log or HOME / "Library/Logs/glaeda-cmux-jobs.jsonl"
    starts: list[float] | None
    try:
        with path.open(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            starts = []
            for line in f:
                if '"started"' not in line:
                    continue
                try:
                    doc = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                at = doc.get("at") if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
                if doc.get("event") == "started" and isinstance(at, (int, float)) and not isinstance(at, bool):
                    starts.append(float(at))
            starts.sort()
    except OSError:
        starts = None
    if log is None:
        _JOB_STARTS, _JOB_STARTS_READ = starts, True
    return starts


def jobs_since_use(fam: "Family", idle_hours: float, now: float | None = None) -> int | None:
    """Host CI jobs started since a hot-tier item's last use, or None (not hot tier, or no job log)."""
    if fam.id not in FLEET_FAMILY_IDS:
        return None
    starts = job_starts()
    if starts is None:
        return None
    last_use = (time.time() if now is None else now) - idle_hours * 3600
    return len(starts) - bisect.bisect_right(starts, last_use)


_RUNNING_JOB_HOURS: float | None = None


def running_job_hours() -> float:
    """Age in hours of the oldest running CI job (Runner.Worker), 0 when none; ps failing counts as a day."""
    global _RUNNING_JOB_HOURS
    if _RUNNING_JOB_HOURS is None:
        try:
            out = subprocess.run(["ps", "-axww", "-o", "etime=,command="], capture_output=True, text=True,
                                 timeout=60).stdout
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            out = None
        oldest = 0.0 if out is not None else 24.0
        for line in (out or "").splitlines():
            etime, _, cmd = line.strip().partition(" ")
            if CI_WORKER.search(cmd):
                days, _, clock = etime.rpartition("-")
                parts = [int(x) for x in clock.split(":") if x.isdigit()]
                secs = sum(v * 60 ** i for i, v in enumerate(reversed(parts))) + int(days or 0) * 86400
                oldest = max(oldest, secs / 3600)
        _RUNNING_JOB_HOURS = oldest
    return _RUNNING_JOB_HOURS


def item_due(fam: "Family", idle_hours: float, window: float) -> tuple[bool, int | None]:
    """Whether an item is old enough to delete, and the job count that decided it (None: wall clock).

    A hot-tier item used since the oldest running CI job started is never due: that job may hold it
    (a `cp -cR` clone leaves the source's times alone, and a step between commands names no path),
    which the old defer-while-CI-runs rule covered. Its own run counts toward jobs_since, so without
    this a job could age the seed it started from past pressure_jobs."""
    jobs = jobs_since_use(fam, idle_hours)
    if ACTIVITY_MODE == "pressure" and fam.id in FLEET_FAMILY_IDS:
        # Pressure spends disposable hot state until the free target, even on a quiet host.
        # A running job may touch a source between commands; cwd, command-line, socket and job-unit
        # checks preserve that veto. Do not use the job's age as a blanket six-hour retention window.
        return idle_hours >= POLICY["pressure_idle_hours"], jobs
    if jobs is None and fam.id == "cmux-pr-builds":
        return False, None
    if jobs is None or ACTIVITY_MODE is None:
        return idle_hours >= window, jobs
    if idle_hours <= running_job_hours():
        return False, jobs
    return (jobs >= POLICY["sweep_jobs"]
            or (idle_hours >= POLICY["soft_idle_hours"] and jobs >= POLICY["pressure_jobs"])), jobs


def host_busy(fleet: Path | None = None, now: float | None = None) -> str:
    """Why this host is not idle, or "": a CI job or build process, a dev-build worker job, an active or
    unreadable reservation, or the fleet host lock held (PR jobs hold it shared, fleet builds exclusive).
    Anything unreadable counts as busy."""
    fleet = FLEET_DIR if fleet is None else fleet
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    try:
        ps = subprocess.run(["ps", "-axww", "-o", "command="], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return "cannot list processes"
    if ps.returncode != 0 or not ps.stdout.strip():
        return "cannot list processes"
    for line in ps.stdout.splitlines():
        if CI_WORKER.search(line):
            return f"a CI job is running ({line.strip()[:120]})"
        if BUILD_PROCESS.search(line):  # the whole line: an Xcode path may hold a space
            return f"a build is running ({line.strip()[:120]})"
    try:
        with os.scandir(fleet / "jobs") as it:
            if any(not e.name.startswith(".") for e in it):
                return f"a dev-build worker job is in {fleet / 'jobs'}"
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    except OSError:
        return f"cannot read {fleet / 'jobs'}"
    try:
        doc = json.loads((fleet / "reservation.json").read_text())
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return "the host reservation is unreadable"
    else:
        # glaeda_reservation's rule: another schema or a non-integer time is invalid, and invalid is active
        until = doc.get("until") if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
        if (not isinstance(doc, dict) or doc.get("schema") != "glaeda-reservation/v1"
                or not isinstance(until, int) or isinstance(until, bool) or now < until):
            return "the host is reserved"
    try:
        fd = os.open(fleet / "host.lock", os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return ""
    except OSError:
        return "the host lock is unreadable"
    try:
        # shared, as the runner hook's host_held probes: an exclusive probe could make a PR job's own
        # one-shot exclusive take fail. PR jobs (shared holders) are caught by Runner.Worker above.
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_SH | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except OSError:
        return "a fleet build holds the host lock"
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    return ""


def idle_due(stamp: Path | None = None, every_s: float = IDLE_SWEEP_EVERY_S) -> bool:
    return not _fresh(IDLE_STAMP if stamp is None else stamp, every_s)


# Xcode copies and simulator runtimes (idle sweep, macOS). An Xcode is an install, not a rebuild: it is only
# reported, with the pins that name it. A runtime is a multi-GiB download; one goes only when nothing uses it.
RUNTIME_IDLE_DAYS = 14.0  # simctl's lastUsedAt: an iOS lane touches its runtime on every run


def _version_key(v: str) -> tuple[int, ...]:
    return tuple(int(x) for x in re.findall(r"[0-9]+", v))


def _iso_age_hours(text: object, now: float) -> float | None:
    if not isinstance(text, str):
        return None
    try:
        import datetime
        at = datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(text.replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return (now - at) / 3600


def runtime_items(runtimes: dict, devices: dict, now: float | None = None) -> list[Item]:
    """Judge simulator runtimes (`simctl runtime list -j`) against devices (`simctl list devices -j`).
    Reclaimable only when deletable, used by no device, not its platform's newest, unused for
    RUNTIME_IDLE_DAYS, and no simulator is booted. Unknown fields keep it."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    by_runtime = devices.get("devices") if isinstance(devices, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(runtimes, dict) or not isinstance(by_runtime, dict):
        return []
    booted = any(isinstance(d, dict) and d.get("state") == "Booted"
                 for devs in by_runtime.values() if isinstance(devs, list) for d in devs)
    newest: dict[str, str] = {}
    for r in runtimes.values():
        if isinstance(r, dict):
            plat, v = str(r.get("platformIdentifier", "")), str(r.get("version", ""))
            if plat not in newest or _version_key(v) > _version_key(newest[plat]):
                newest[plat] = v
    items = []
    for rid, r in sorted(runtimes.items()):
        if not isinstance(r, dict):
            continue
        reasons = [f"{r.get('version', '?')} ({r.get('build', '?')}) {r.get('state', '?')}"]
        keep = []
        devs = by_runtime.get(r.get("runtimeIdentifier"), [])
        if not r.get("runtimeIdentifier") or devs:
            keep.append(f"{len(devs) if isinstance(devs, list) else '?'} simulator devices use it")
        if newest.get(str(r.get("platformIdentifier", ""))) == str(r.get("version", "")):
            keep.append("its platform's newest")
        if r.get("deletable") is not True:
            keep.append("simctl cannot delete it")
        idle = _iso_age_hours(r.get("lastUsedAt"), now)
        if idle is None:
            keep.append("no last-use time")
        if booted:
            keep.append("a simulator is booted (deleting restarts CoreSimulatorService)")
        size = r.get("sizeBytes")
        verdict = ("kept" if keep else "recent" if idle is not None and idle < RUNTIME_IDLE_DAYS * 24
                   else "reclaimable")
        items.append(Item("simulator-runtimes", str(rid), size if isinstance(size, int) else 0,
                          round(idle or 0.0, 1), verdict, reasons + keep))
    return items


def simctl_json(*args: str) -> dict | None:
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["xcrun", "simctl", *args, "-j"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        return json.loads(r.stdout) if r.returncode == 0 else None
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, ValueError):
        return None


def simulator_runtimes() -> list[Item]:
    if not DARWIN or not shutil.which("xcrun"):
        return []
    runtimes, devices = simctl_json("runtime", "list"), simctl_json("list", "devices")
    return runtime_items(runtimes, devices) if runtimes is not None and devices is not None else []


XCODE_PIN_RE = r"\s--toolchain-xcode[\s=]+(\S+)"


def xcode_pins(home: Path = HOME) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Xcode app paths something names -> what: xcode-select, and a runner hook's --toolchain-xcode."""
    pins: dict[str, str] = {}
    for hook in home.glob(RUNNER_HOOKS):
        try:
            for app in re.findall(XCODE_PIN_RE, hook.read_text()):
                pins[os.path.realpath(app.strip("'\""))] = "a runner hook's --toolchain-xcode"
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            continue
    try:
        sel = subprocess.run(["xcode-select", "-p"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10).stdout.strip()
        if ".app/" in sel:
            pins.setdefault(os.path.realpath(sel.split(".app/")[0] + ".app"), "xcode-select")
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass
    return pins


def xcode_items(apps: Path = Path("/Applications"), pins: dict[str, str] | None = None) -> list[Item]:
    """Every Xcode*.app, report-only, with the pin that names it or why nothing here removes it."""
    pins = xcode_pins() if pins is None else pins
    items = []
    for app in sorted(apps.glob("Xcode*.app")):
        if app.is_symlink():
            continue
        try:
            import plistlib
            version = plistlib.loads((app / "Contents/Info.plist").read_bytes()).get("CFBundleShortVersionString", "?")
            owner = pwd.getpwuid(app.stat().st_uid).pw_name
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001  (a report line, never a decision)
            version, owner = "?", "?"
        pin = pins.get(os.path.realpath(app))
        why = [f"Xcode {version}, owned by {owner}",
               f"pinned by {pin}" if pin else "no pin names it: remove it by hand if no workflow uses it"]
        items.append(Item("xcode-apps", str(app), du_bytes(app), 0.0, "report-only", why))
    return items


def apply_runtimes(items: list[Item], receipt: Path, halt=None) -> int:
    """Delete reclaimable runtimes after judging them again from a fresh simctl reading."""
    released = 0
    for i in items:
        if i.verdict != "reclaimable":
            continue
        why = halt() if halt is not None else ""
        fresh = {x.path: x for x in simulator_runtimes()}.get(i.path)
        if why:
            outcome = f"stopped:{why[:60]}"
        elif fresh is None or fresh.verdict != "reclaimable":
            outcome = "changed:in-use"
        else:
            try:
                r = subprocess.run(["xcrun", "simctl", "runtime", "delete", i.path], capture_output=True,
                                   text=True, timeout=900)
                outcome = "reclaimed" if r.returncode == 0 else f"incomplete:exit {r.returncode}"
            except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
                outcome = f"incomplete:{e.__class__.__name__}"
            if outcome == "reclaimed":
                released += i.bytes
        rec = {"at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z"), "host": platform.node(), "outcome": outcome,
               "rebuild": "xcodebuild -downloadPlatform or Xcode's Components settings", **asdict(i),
               "sweep": "idle"}
        receipt.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with receipt.open("a") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps(rec) + "\n")
        print(f"  {outcome:16s} {gib(i.bytes)}  simulator runtime {i.path}")
        if why:
            break
    return released


def seed_verdict(path: Path) -> str:
    """Why a seed stays whatever its age, or "": one of its root's newest SEED_KEEP seeds of the prefix the
    root's last job recorded (seed-source.json), or of any prefix when it recorded none. Dot entries are a
    download or delete in progress, never a seed."""
    try:
        prefix = json.loads((path.parent.parent / SEED_SOURCE).read_text()).get("prefix") or ""
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        prefix = ""
    if not isinstance(prefix, str):
        prefix = ""
    if path.name.startswith(".") or not path.name.startswith(prefix):
        return ""
    current = []
    for c in _entries(path.parent):
        if c.name.startswith(prefix) and not c.name.startswith(".") and c.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):
            try:
                current.append((c.stat(follow_symlinks=False).st_mtime, c.name))
            except OSError:
                continue
    keep = 1 if ACTIVITY_MODE == "pressure" else SEED_KEEP
    newest = {name for _, name in sorted(current, reverse=True)[:keep]}
    return f"one of its root's newest {keep} current seeds" if path.name in newest else ""


WORKTREE_DIRS = ("worktrees", "cmux-worktrees", "glaeda-worktrees")
CHECKOUT_BUILD_IDLE_HOURS = 24.0


def _checkout_git(checkout: Path, *args: str) -> list[str]:
    """NUL-separated output of a read-only git command in checkout, or [] on any failure. The caller's
    GIT_* variables would override -C (a hook's GIT_DIR names one repository for every checkout)."""
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if not k.startswith("GIT_")}
    env.update(GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS="0", GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT="0")
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", os.fspath(checkout), *args], capture_output=True, timeout=60, env=env)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return []
    return [os.fsdecode(f) for f in r.stdout.split(b"\0") if f] if r.returncode == 0 else []


def project_checkouts(projects: Path) -> list[Path]:
    """Every checkout under projects, by real path: each repository directly below it and its linked
    worktrees there. Worktrees outside projects (a Claude session's scratchpad) belong to their own family."""
    real = os.path.realpath(projects)
    repos = [c for c in _children(projects) if (c / ".git").exists() and not c.is_symlink()]
    found = {os.path.realpath(c) for c in repos}
    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=8) as pool:
        for fields in pool.map(lambda c: _checkout_git(c, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain", "-z"), repos):
            for f in fields:
                if f.startswith("worktree "):
                    w = os.path.realpath(f[len("worktree "):])
                    if w.startswith(real + "/") and os.path.isdir(w):
                        found.add(w)
    return [Path(w) for w in sorted(found)]


def _checkout_build_dirs(checkout: Path) -> list[Path]:
    found = []
    tracked = _checkout_git(checkout, "ls-files", "-z", "--", "Package.swift", ":(glob)**/Package.swift")
    for rel in sorted({os.path.dirname(r) for r in tracked}):
        build = checkout / rel / ".build"
        if not build.is_symlink() and _head(os.fspath(build / "CACHEDIR.TAG"), 64).startswith(CACHEDIR_TAG):
            found.append(build)
    found += [g for g in _children(checkout / ".glaeda/apple-build/cache") if g.is_dir() and not g.is_symlink()]
    return found


def checkout_builds(projects: Path) -> list[Path]:
    """The build output inside the checkouts under projects that its tool recreates: SwiftPM's .build
    (tagged with a CACHEDIR.TAG) beside each tracked Package.swift, and each glaeda-apple cache generation
    (.glaeda/apple-build/cache/<key>; the receipts beside it stay). The index names the packages, so
    nothing walks the checkouts; the git calls run in parallel (hundreds of checkouts on a laptop)."""
    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=8) as pool:
        return [b for bs in pool.map(_checkout_build_dirs, project_checkouts(projects)) for b in bs]


def checkout_build_family(projects: Path) -> Family:
    return Family("checkout-build", projects, True,
                  "swift build or glaeda-apple warm rebuilds it; also counted in its checkout",
                  checkout_builds=True, build_output=True, min_idle_hours=CHECKOUT_BUILD_IDLE_HOURS,
                  owner="SwiftPM and glaeda-apple, per checkout", rebuild_minutes=15)


def apple_build_verdict(path: Path) -> str:
    """Why a glaeda-apple cache generation stays whatever its age, or "": a run is in flight or holds the
    build lock. Xcode writes deeper than the idle check looks, so the lock is what shows a build."""
    state = path.parent.parent
    if (state / "inflight.json").exists():
        return "a glaeda-apple run is in flight"
    try:
        fd = os.open(state / "build.lock", os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW)
    except OSError:
        return ""
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_SH | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
        return ""
    except OSError:
        return "a glaeda-apple run holds its build lock"
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def worktree_roots(projects: Path) -> list[Path]:
    """Worktree parents under ~/Projects: the fixed names plus any `<name>-worktrees`."""
    names = set(WORKTREE_DIRS)
    for c in _children(projects):
        if c.name.endswith("-worktrees") and c.is_dir() and not c.is_symlink():
            names.add(c.name)
    return [projects / n for n in sorted(names)]


def fs_type(path: Path, mounts: Path = Path("/proc/mounts")) -> str:
    """Filesystem type of the mount holding path ("" when unknown, e.g. on macOS)."""
    try:
        target = str(mount_point(path))
        best = ""
        for line in mounts.read_text().splitlines():
            f = line.split()
            if len(f) >= 3 and f[1].replace("\\040", " ") == target:
                best = f[2]  # a later mount on the same point shadows earlier ones
        return best
    except OSError:
        return ""


def darwin_user_tmp() -> Path | None:
    """The per-user temp dir macOS gives every process as $TMPDIR (/var/folders/../T), resolved.

    It is not /private/tmp, so scratch that tools create with mktemp or tempfile lands here
    unseen: 14.5 GiB of throwaway cmux copies and build traces sat in it on Air Blue (2026-09-25).
    """
    out = subprocess.run(["getconf", "DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    value = out.stdout.strip() if out.returncode == 0 else ""
    if not value:
        return None
    path = Path(os.path.realpath(value))
    return path if path.is_dir() and path != Path("/private/tmp") else None


def bazel_family(root: Path) -> Family:
    # Bazel's output root on macOS (Linux keeps it under ~/.cache/bazel, a bazel-output-root layout). One output
    # base per workspace (an md5 name) and the unpacked install/: all rebuilt by bazel, and external/
    # holds its repository fetches, so git inside is fetched, not work (as in DerivedData). A running
    # bazel server holds its output base open and names it on its command line, so the in-use checks
    # keep it; a `bazel run` binary writing into its runfiles is kept by its cwd while it runs.
    # cache/ is the repository cache, not a rebuild: its downloads sit too deep for the idle check,
    # no process names it, newer Bazel links output bases' external/ into it, and it is what an
    # offline build or a vanished URL needs. install/ bases carry future mtimes, so they read recent.
    return Family("bazel-output", root, True, "bazel rebuilds it", skip=("cache",), owner="bazel",
                  rebuild_minutes=30, build_output=True)


def default_families() -> list[Family]:
    tmp = Path("/private/tmp") if DARWIN else Path("/tmp")
    tmpfs = tmp.is_dir() and fs_type(tmp) == "tmpfs"
    uid = os.getuid()
    projects = HOME / "Projects"
    wt_roots = worktree_roots(projects)
    fams = [
        Family("xcode-derived-data", HOME / "Library/Developer/Xcode/DerivedData", True,
               "next xcodebuild rebuilds it", owner="Xcode, per checkout", rebuild_minutes=15,
               build_output=True),
        Family("claude-scratchpad", tmp / f"claude-{uid}", True,
               "session scratch space; the session is idle", depth=2,
               skip=("bundled-skills", "bash-edit-diff"), owner="Claude sessions", rebuild_minutes=0.5,
               # sessions clone into <session>/scratchpad/<name>; each clone must lose nothing
               git_disposable=True, nested_git=True),
        Family("tmp", tmp, True, "agent scratch under the temp dir",
               skip=(f"claude-{uid}",), files=True,
               min_bytes=1024**2 if tmpfs else None, git_disposable=True, owner="agents and tools",
               rebuild_minutes=0.5),
        # cmux's cloud reload and CI jobs keep warm trees per unit (reload-cloud/{cmux-base,work},
        # reload-cloud-ios/{DerivedData,work}); a build mini grew 36 GiB here within hours. Depth 2
        # so a unit's DerivedData can go (see derived_data) while the clones below cmux-base and work
        # stay protected; hq's cleanup-host.sh ages those out.
        Family("cmux-job-cache", HOME / ".cache/cmux-job", True,
               "next cmux reload or CI job rebuilds it (cold)", depth=2, git_disposable=True,
               owner="cmuxterm-hq reload-cloud scripts", rebuild_minutes=30),
        # Caches are disposable by definition; an idle one goes under pressure, a live one (go-build
        # while a Go build runs) stays recent. Any git data inside keeps it, as everywhere else.
        Family("user-cache", HOME / ".cache", True, "the tool re-downloads or rebuilds it",
               # glaeda* holds glaeda-gh daemon state, the minis' native cache root, fleet-cas deployments
               # and benchmark receipts; huggingface keeps its token; codex-runtimes is an install
               skip=("cmux-job", "huggingface", "codex-runtimes"), skip_prefixes=("glaeda",),
               owner="tools (XDG cache)", rebuild_minutes=5),
        Family("library-caches", HOME / "Library/Caches", True, "the app re-downloads or rebuilds it",
               # glaeda: OTA candidates and LAN seed stages; PassKit: web service cookies; tidy-branches:
               # a deletion receipt; the rest are Apple daemons without the com.apple. prefix
               skip=("glaeda", "PassKit", "tidy-branches", "CloudKit", "GeoServices", "FamilyCircle",
                     "familycircled", "GameKit", "askpermissiond", "mbuseragent", "AMSDataMigratorTool",
                     "PHTPhenotype", "SharedImageCache", "Animoji", "SiriTTS", "GIPPseudonymousID"),
               skip_prefixes=("com.apple.",), owner="apps", rebuild_minutes=5),
        # Sealed caches (cache_layout) anywhere else in the home directory: ~/.npm/_cacache,
        # ~/.bun/install/cache, ~/.cargo/registry, ~/go/pkg/mod, rustup toolchains. Found by content,
        # so a tool this list never heard of is covered once its cache has a recognized layout.
        # A day at least: Linux's relatime moves a file's access time once a day, so a cache read every
        # hour can show a day-old atime.
        Family("home-caches", HOME, True, "the tool re-downloads or rebuilds it", discover=True,
               owner="tools (found by cache layout)", rebuild_minutes=5, min_idle_hours=24.0),
        Family("simulators", HOME / "Library/Developer/CoreSimulator", False,
               "use `xcrun simctl delete unavailable` (report only)"),
        Family("projects", projects, False, "checkouts (report only)",
               skip=tuple(r.name for r in wt_roots)),
    ]
    user_tmp = darwin_user_tmp() if DARWIN else None
    if user_tmp is not None:
        # Apple daemons keep live state in com.apple.* entries without holding them open.
        fams.append(Family("user-tmp", user_tmp, True, "agent scratch under the per-user $TMPDIR",
                           skip_prefixes=("com.apple.",), git_disposable=True, bulk_sizes=True,
                           owner="agents and tools", rebuild_minutes=0.5))
    # The dev-build worker's shared cache (the cmuxterm-hq controller's worker, live only on the dev-build worker
    # host since cmuxterm-hq#661): visible with its owner, never deleted here. When the worker is unloaded the
    # report says so, since other tools may still read parts of it (2026-09-24).
    fams.append(Family("hq-build-fleet-cache", Path("/Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/cache"), True,
                       "the retired dev-build worker rebuilds its cache", retired_only=True,
                       owner="cmux dev-build worker (hq controller)",
                       owner_job="system/ai.manaflow.cmux-build-worker"))
    bazel_user = ""
    with contextlib.suppress(KeyError):
        bazel_user = pwd.getpwuid(uid).pw_name
    if DARWIN and bazel_user:
        fams.append(bazel_family(Path(f"/private/var/tmp/_bazel_{bazel_user}")))
    if DARWIN:  # the owned cmux minis
        fams += fleet_families()
        for runner in HOME.glob("actions-runner*"):
            temp = runner / "_work/_temp"
            if (runner / ".runner").is_file() and (runner / "glaeda-hooks").is_dir() and temp.is_dir():
                fams.append(Family("ci-derived-data", temp, True, "the CI test job rebuilds it",
                                   include=("cmux-derived-data-tests-",), build_output=True,
                                   owner="cmux CI test jobs", rebuild_minutes=6))
        # Bounded by its owner: node_product_cache.py evicts LRU to CMUX_NODE_PRODUCT_CACHE_MAX_BYTES (24 GiB).
        fams.append(Family("cmux-node-products", Path("/Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/node-products"), False,
                           "report only: its owner evicts LRU to a 24 GiB budget",
                           owner="cmux node_product_cache.py"))
    for root in wt_roots:
        fams.append(Family("worktrees", root, False,
                           "run glaeda-worktree-reclaim-all (report only)"))
    # SwiftPM and glaeda-apple build output inside the checkouts: every other family here keeps a checkout
    # whole, so under pressure nothing was left to evict while agent sessions compiled packages in hundreds
    # of worktrees (Air Blue fell to 134 MiB free, 2026-09-28). A day idle, since the session may come back.
    fams.append(checkout_build_family(projects))
    # Cargo owns target validity and RFC #1137 owns Cargo reclaim, so this is visibility only.
    # Sizes here are also counted inside their checkout under projects or worktrees.
    fams.append(Family("cargo-target", projects, False,
                       "report only: cargo clean; also counted in its checkout",
                       depth=4, match="target", marker="CACHEDIR.TAG"))
    return [f for f in fams if f.root.is_dir()]


@dataclass
class Item:
    family: str
    path: str
    bytes: int
    idle_hours: float
    verdict: str  # reclaimable | recent | in-use | git-checkout | unchecked | report-only | kept
    reasons: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    jobs_since: int | None = None  # host CI jobs started since its last use (activity_due), when counted


def du_bytes(path: Path) -> int:
    out = subprocess.run(["du", "-xsk", str(path)], capture_output=True, text=True)
    try:
        return int(out.stdout.split()[0]) * 1024
    except (IndexError, ValueError):
        return 0


BULK_SIZE_MIN = 64  # unsized candidates in a bulk_sizes family before one walk beats per-item du


def du_children(root: Path) -> dict[str, int]:
    """Sizes of every directory directly below root from a single walk, keyed like candidates."""
    out = subprocess.run(["du", "-xk", "-d", "1", str(root)], capture_output=True, text=True)
    sizes: dict[str, int] = {}
    for line in out.stdout.splitlines():
        fields = line.split(None, 1)
        if len(fields) == 2 and fields[1] != str(root) and fields[0].isdigit():
            sizes[str(root / Path(fields[1]).name)] = int(fields[0]) * 1024
    return sizes


def newest_mtime(path: Path, levels: int = 2, budget: int = 3000, access: bool = False) -> float:
    """Newest mtime of path and entries up to `levels` below it, visiting at most `budget` entries.

    Build tools touch top-level files (DerivedData info.plist, logs) on every run, so a shallow,
    bounded look is enough to see activity without walking the whole tree. With `access`, a file's
    access time counts too, where the filesystem keeps it: a cache is read far more often than written.
    It is a hint for LRU order, not protection: APFS moves atime lazily and Linux relatime at most daily,
    and only the top `levels` are seen (npm's cacache and Go's module cache keep their blobs deeper).
    A cache in use is kept by the process evidence (open files, cwd, command lines), not by atime.
    Only files count, and never the ones a layout test reads (LAYOUT_PROBES): listing a directory or
    reading a probe would move the very times being judged.
    """
    try:
        newest = path.lstat().st_mtime
    except OSError:
        return 0.0
    frontier = [str(path)]
    for _ in range(levels):
        nxt: list[str] = []
        for d in frontier:
            try:
                with os.scandir(d) as it:
                    for e in it:
                        if budget <= 0:
                            return newest
                        budget -= 1
                        try:
                            st = e.stat(follow_symlinks=False)
                        except OSError:
                            continue
                        newest = max(newest, st.st_mtime)
                        if access and stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) and e.name not in LAYOUT_PROBES:
                            newest = max(newest, st.st_atime)
                        if stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
                            nxt.append(e.path)
            except OSError:
                pass
        frontier = nxt
    return newest


def item_mtime(fam: Family, path: Path) -> float:
    """An item's last use as survey and apply judge it: its newest mtime near the top, and for SwiftPM scratch
    also its lock, which owned_spm_scratch touches whenever a job links the directory."""
    newest = newest_mtime(path, 2 if fam.reclaimable else 1, 3000 if fam.reclaimable else 300, access=fam.discover)
    if fam.id == SPM_SCRATCH:
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            newest = max(newest, spm_lock(path).stat().st_mtime)
    return newest


class NoEvidence(Exception):
    """Process evidence could not be collected; nothing may be deleted."""


def process_evidence() -> tuple[list[str], str]:
    """Every path a process has open (cwd and files) plus all command lines.

    Raises NoEvidence when either source is unavailable, so callers fail closed.
    """
    opened: list[str] = []
    proc = Path("/proc")
    if not DARWIN and proc.is_dir():
        for pid in proc.iterdir():
            if not pid.name.isdigit():
                continue
            try:  # other users' processes are unreadable; that is expected, not a finding
                fds = [pid / "fd" / fd for fd in os.listdir(pid / "fd")]
            except OSError:
                fds = []
            for link in [pid / "cwd", pid / "exe", *fds]:  # exe: a running rustc from a toolchain
                try:
                    opened.append(os.readlink(link))
                except OSError:
                    pass
        if not opened:  # at least this process has a cwd, so empty means /proc is unusable
            raise NoEvidence("/proc showed no open paths")
    else:
        lsof = shutil.which("lsof") or "/usr/sbin/lsof"
        try:
            out = subprocess.run([lsof, "-n", "-P", "-Fn"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
            raise NoEvidence(f"lsof: {e}") from e
        opened = [line[1:] for line in out.stdout.splitlines() if line.startswith("n/")]
        if not opened:
            raise NoEvidence(f"lsof returned nothing (exit {out.returncode})")
    try:
        ps = subprocess.run(["ps", "-axww", "-o", "command="], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        raise NoEvidence(f"ps: {e}") from e
    if ps.returncode != 0 or not ps.stdout.strip():
        raise NoEvidence(f"ps failed (exit {ps.returncode})")
    return opened, command_text(ps.stdout)


def command_lines() -> str:
    """Current command lines (command_text), or "" when ps fails."""
    try:
        ps = subprocess.run(["ps", "-axww", "-o", "command="], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return ""
    return command_text(ps.stdout) if ps.returncode == 0 else ""


def command_text(ps_output: str) -> str:
    """Command lines with repeated slashes collapsed: $TMPDIR ends in a slash, so a script's
    "$TMPDIR/x" reads .../T//x, which would not match the path it names."""
    return re.sub(r"/{2,}", "/", ps_output)


def spellings(path: str) -> list[str]:
    """The same location as processes may name it (macOS /tmp and /var are symlinks into /private)."""
    out = [path]
    for real, alias in (("/private/tmp", "/tmp"), ("/private/var", "/var"), ("/private/etc", "/etc")):
        if path == real or path.startswith(real + "/"):
            out.append(alias + path[len(real):])
    return out


def named_by(path: str, cmds: str) -> bool:
    for spelling in spellings(path):
        if spelling in cmds and re.search(
                re.escape(spelling) + r"(?=$|[/\s'\"=:,;)\]])", cmds, re.M):
            return True
    return False


def open_index(opened: list[str]) -> frozenset[str]:
    """Every opened path and all its ancestors, so in_cwd is one lookup per spelling instead of a
    scan of every open file (lsof lists tens of thousands; the scan cost ~1 min per survey)."""
    out: set[str] = set()
    for o in opened:
        while o and o not in out:  # an ancestor already present has all of its own ancestors too
            out.add(o)
            o = o.rpartition("/")[0]
    return frozenset(out)


def in_cwd(path: str, opened: list[str] | frozenset[str]) -> bool:
    """True when any process has its cwd or an open file inside path."""
    index = opened if isinstance(opened, frozenset) else open_index(opened)
    return any(sp in index for sp in spellings(path))


def claude_config_dirs() -> list[Path]:
    """~/.claude plus alternate config dirs (~/.claude-<account>, $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR)."""
    dirs = [HOME / ".claude", *sorted(HOME.glob(".claude-*"))]
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"):
        dirs.append(Path(os.environ["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"]))
    return [d for d in dirs if d.is_dir()]


def claude_session_active(session_id: str, idle_s: float) -> bool:
    for config in claude_config_dirs():
        for transcript in (config / "projects").glob(f"*/{session_id}.jsonl"):
            try:
                if time.time() - transcript.stat().st_mtime < idle_s:
                    return True
            except OSError:
                continue
    return False


DELETING_PREFIX = ".glaeda-disk-deleting-"  # remove() renames a tree to this before deleting it


def derived_data(fam: Family, path: Path) -> bool:
    """Build output despite git inside: Xcode DerivedData (SourcePackages holds clones), and a tree
    remove() had already renamed aside when it was interrupted, which is only ever reclaimed. Neither
    is searched for git. A tool's own cache is searched, but git_state does not enter its sealed parts."""
    return bool(fam.build_output
            or re.fullmatch(re.escape(DELETING_PREFIX) + r".+-[0-9]+", path.name)
            or (fam.id == "cmux-job-cache" and path.name == "DerivedData"))


# Cache layouts, recognized by what a tool writes rather than by a directory's name. A tree that
# matches one is "sealed": the tool alone writes below its top level (content-addressed blobs,
# unpacked downloads, fetched clones), so git_state checks only that top level and the one below it,
# where a person's clone would sit, instead of walking millions of files it cannot finish (the Go build
# cache held 18 GiB and far more than the 20000-entry budget on Air Blue, 2026-09-27). Anything else is
# searched in full and stays "unchecked" when the search cannot finish. Sealing only shortens the git
# search in families whose items are caches by where they sit; a layout is never enough to delete
# something on its own except in discovery, and only for DISCOVERABLE layouts.
CACHEDIR_TAG = b"Signature: 8a477f597d28d172789f06886806bc55"  # https://bford.info/cachedir/
# Chromium's blockfile cache index starts with kIndexMagic 0xC103CAC3, little endian.
CHROMIUM_INDEX_MAGIC = b"\xc3\xca\x03\xc1"
CHROMIUM_SIMPLE_ENTRY = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{16}_[0-9s]")
HEX_SHARD = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{1,2}")
BUN_ENTRY = re.compile(r".+@[^@/]*@@@[0-9]+")  # bun's install cache: <name>@<version>@@@<n>
MD5_NAME = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{32}")
# Version-control state: a directory holding one is a checkout, never a cache and never searched past.
VCS_MARKERS = (".git", ".jj", ".hg", ".sl", ".svn")
SEALED_BUDGET = 500_000  # entries git_state reads at the tops of sealed caches, per call
LAYOUT_LISTING = 4096  # entries a layout test reads from one directory
SHARD_SAMPLE = 16  # entries sampled from each of the first four shards; all must be blobs or shards
# Files a layout test reads. Reading moves their access time (Linux relatime), so the LRU scan
# (newest_mtime with access) ignores their atime or every recognized cache would look just used.
LAYOUT_PROBES = frozenset({"CACHEDIR.TAG", "index"})


def _is_file(p: str) -> bool:
    return os.path.isfile(p) and not os.path.islink(p)


def _is_dir(p: str) -> bool:
    return os.path.isdir(p) and not os.path.islink(p)


def _head(p: str, n: int) -> bytes:
    """The first n bytes of a regular file, or b"". Never opens a FIFO or device, which could block."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(p)
        if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            return b""
        fd = os.open(p, os.O_RDONLY | getattr(os, "O_NOATIME", 0) | getattr(os, "O_NONBLOCK", 0))
    except OSError:
        return b""
    try:
        return os.read(fd, n)
    except OSError:
        return b""
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def _listing(d: str, limit: int = LAYOUT_LISTING, partial: bool = False) -> list[os.DirEntry] | None:
    """Up to `limit` entries of d, or None when d is unreadable or, unless `partial`, holds more."""
    out: list[os.DirEntry] = []
    try:
        with os.scandir(d) as it:
            for e in it:
                if len(out) >= limit:
                    return out if partial else None
                out.append(e)
    except OSError:
        return None
    return out


def vcs_at(d: str) -> bool:
    return any(os.path.lexists(os.path.join(d, m)) for m in VCS_MARKERS) or looks_like_repository(d)


def _hash_sharded(d: str, entries: list[os.DirEntry]) -> bool:
    """A content-addressed store: at least 16 entries, nine in ten of them 1-2 hex digit shard
    directories of one width with some a-f (Go's build cache, pip's http cache, ccache), and the sampled
    contents of the first shards all blobs or deeper shards. A git objects directory looks the same; it
    is never judged here, since git_state and discovery stop at the repository around it."""
    if len(entries) < 16 or os.path.basename(d) == "objects" or looks_like_repository(os.path.dirname(d)):
        return False
    shards = [e for e in entries if HEX_SHARD.fullmatch(e.name) and e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False)]
    # one width, and some a-f: numbered directories (Maven's 10..35 versions) are not hex shards
    if (len(shards) * 10 < len(entries) * 9 or len({len(e.name) for e in shards}) != 1
            or not any(re.search("[a-f]", e.name) for e in shards)):
        return False
    for shard in sorted(shards, key=lambda e: e.name)[:4]:
        inner = _listing(shard.path, SHARD_SAMPLE, partial=True)
        if inner is None:
            return False
        for e in inner:
            # blobs, or deeper shards (pip's http cache nests a/b/c/d/e/<hash>)
            if not (e.is_file(follow_symlinks=False)
                    or (e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False) and HEX_SHARD.fullmatch(e.name))):
                return False
    return True


def _chromium_cache(d: str) -> bool:
    """A Chromium disk cache: the simple cache (index-dir/the-real-index beside <16 hex>_0 entries) or
    the blockfile cache (an index carrying Chromium's magic, and data_0 to data_3). An index and a data_0
    alone are any dataset's names."""
    if _is_file(os.path.join(d, "index-dir", "the-real-index")):
        return any(CHROMIUM_SIMPLE_ENTRY.fullmatch(e.name) for e in _listing(d, 64, partial=True) or [])
    return (_head(os.path.join(d, "index"), 4) == CHROMIUM_INDEX_MAGIC
            and all(_is_file(os.path.join(d, f"data_{i}")) for i in range(4)))


def stat_layout(d: str) -> str:
    """The layout d matches by a few files a tool writes at its top, or "" (see listing_layout)."""
    if _head(os.path.join(d, "CACHEDIR.TAG"), len(CACHEDIR_TAG)) == CACHEDIR_TAG:
        return "cachedir-tag"  # cargo's registry, uv, Gradle and others declare their caches so
    j = lambda *parts: os.path.join(d, *parts)  # noqa: E731
    if _is_dir(j("index-v5")) and _is_dir(j("content-v2")):
        return "npm-cacache"
    if _is_file(j("lib", "rustlib", "multirust-channel-manifest.toml")) and _is_file(j("lib", "rustlib", "components")):
        return "rustup-toolchain"
    if _is_dir(j("cache", "download")) and _is_file(j("cache", "lock")):
        return "go-module-cache"
    if _chromium_cache(d):
        return "chromium-disk-cache"
    return ""


def listing_layout(d: str, entries: list[os.DirEntry]) -> str:
    """The layout d matches by the names of its entries, or ""."""
    if _hash_sharded(d, entries):
        return "hash-sharded-store"
    names = [e.name for e in entries]
    bun = sum(1 for n in names if BUN_ENTRY.fullmatch(n))
    if bun >= 8 and bun * 2 >= sum(1 for n in names if not n.startswith((".", "@"))):
        return "bun-install-cache"
    # Bazel's output user root: install/ plus md5-named output bases (~/.cache/bazel/_bazel_$USER),
    # each holding bazel's own server/, execroot/ or external/
    if "install" in names and any(
            MD5_NAME.fullmatch(e.name) and e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False)
            and any(_is_dir(os.path.join(e.path, n)) for n in ("server", "execroot", "external"))
            for e in entries):
        return "bazel-output-root"
    return ""


def cache_layout(path: Path | str) -> str:
    """The cache layout a directory matches, or "": never a checkout or repository itself."""
    d = os.fspath(path)
    if not _is_dir(d) or vcs_at(d):
        return ""
    if (found := stat_layout(d)):
        return found
    entries = _listing(d)
    return listing_layout(d, entries) if entries is not None else ""


REPRODUCIBLE_DIRS = frozenset({"node_modules", ".build", "DerivedData", "target", ".venv",
                               "__pycache__", ".gradle", "Pods"})


# Never searched by discover_caches, relative to the home directory: secrets and credentials,
# keychains (under Library), the roots other families own (~/.cache, ~/Library/Caches, ~/Projects), the
# folders macOS guards with a privacy prompt, which a LaunchAgent must not trip and which hold no tool's
# cache, and installed environments that carry a CACHEDIR.TAG without being recreatable (uv tags every
# venv and tool environment it makes; pipx and virtualenvwrapper keep installs). SECRET_NAME drops any
# directory named like a credential store at any depth.
HOME_NEVER = (".ssh", ".gnupg", ".secrets", ".aws", ".azure", ".kube", ".docker", ".password-store",
              ".config/gh", ".local/share/keyrings", ".local/share/uv", ".local/share/pipx",
              ".local/pipx", ".virtualenvs", ".pyenv", ".conda", "miniconda3", "anaconda3",
              ".cache", ".Trash", "Library", "Projects",
              "Desktop", "Documents", "Downloads", "Movies", "Music", "Pictures", "Public", "Applications")
SECRET_NAME = re.compile(r"secret|credential|token|keychain|keyring|password", re.I)
# a project's package and build directories belong to it, not to a tool; ~/.gradle is Gradle's own home
DISCOVER_SKIP = (REPRODUCIBLE_DIRS - {".gradle"}) | {".direnv", "venv", "env"} | set(VCS_MARKERS)
# What discovery may delete. A CACHEDIR.TAG only says "do not back this up": uv writes one into every
# venv, direnv into .direnv. So a tagged directory counts only when its tag names a tool that refetches
# it (TAG_CREATORS) and it holds no Python environment. A hex-sharded store never counts on its own: a
# backup repository (restic's data/) or an app's only copy of its blobs looks the same. A Chromium disk
# cache sits inside a browser profile, which the browser owns.
DISCOVERABLE = ("cachedir-tag", "npm-cacache", "rustup-toolchain", "go-module-cache", "bun-install-cache",
                "bazel-output-root")
TAG_CREATORS = re.compile(rb"^# This file is a cache directory tag created by (cargo|Gradle)\.?\s*$", re.M)
DISCOVER_DEPTH = 5  # deep enough for ~/.rustup/toolchains/<tc>, ~/go/pkg/mod, ~/.bun/install/cache
DISCOVER_BUDGET = 8000  # entries read below each top-level home directory
DISCOVER_SECONDS = 30.0  # and the whole discovery stops after this long; it only ever misses caches


def discoverable(d: str) -> str:
    """The layout that lets discovery delete d, or ""."""
    layout = cache_layout(d)
    if layout not in DISCOVERABLE:
        return ""
    if os.path.lexists(os.path.join(d, "pyvenv.cfg")) or os.path.lexists(os.path.join(d, "conda-meta")):
        return ""
    if layout == "cachedir-tag" and not TAG_CREATORS.search(_head(os.path.join(d, "CACHEDIR.TAG"), 512)):
        return ""
    # cargo tags its target directory too; one outside a project (CARGO_TARGET_DIR) is a build, not the
    # registry or git cache cargo refetches
    if layout == "cachedir-tag" and any(os.path.lexists(os.path.join(d, n))
                                        for n in ("release", "debug", ".rustc_info.json")):
        return ""
    return layout


def discover_caches(root: Path, depth: int = DISCOVER_DEPTH, budget: int = DISCOVER_BUDGET,
                    seconds: float = DISCOVER_SECONDS) -> list[Path]:
    """Every discoverable cache (discoverable) up to `depth` levels below root, outermost only.

    Never entered: symlinks, other filesystems, HOME_NEVER, credential-named directories, the Chromium
    trees (never_delete), a checkout or repository of any VCS, Python environments, and package or
    build-output directories (they belong to the project around them). Each top-level directory gets its
    own `budget` of entries and the whole walk stops after `seconds`, so one huge tree can hide caches,
    never delete anything. The deadline is checked between directories: it cannot interrupt one syscall
    hung on a dead network mount (st_dev keeps discovery off other filesystems, but the first lstat of
    the mount point itself can still block).
    """
    never = {os.path.realpath(root / n) for n in HOME_NEVER}
    deadline = time.monotonic() + seconds
    try:
        dev = root.stat().st_dev
    except OSError:
        return []
    found: list[Path] = []
    for top in _entries(root):
        if not top.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):
            continue
        left = budget
        level = [top.path]
        for _ in range(depth):
            nxt: list[str] = []
            for d in level:
                if time.monotonic() > deadline:
                    return found
                name = os.path.basename(d)
                try:
                    other_fs = os.lstat(d).st_dev != dev
                except OSError:
                    continue
                if (other_fs or os.path.realpath(d) in never or SECRET_NAME.search(name)
                        or name in DISCOVER_SKIP or never_delete(Path(d)) or vcs_at(d)
                        or os.path.lexists(os.path.join(d, "pyvenv.cfg"))):
                    continue
                if stat_layout(d):
                    if discoverable(d):
                        found.append(Path(d))
                    continue  # a sealed cache discovery may not take is not searched either
                entries: list[os.DirEntry] = []
                try:
                    with os.scandir(d) as it:
                        for e in it:
                            left -= 1
                            if left < 0:
                                break
                            entries.append(e)
                except OSError:
                    continue
                if left < 0:
                    break  # a partial listing can hide a layout, never invent one: stop here
                if listing_layout(d, entries):
                    if discoverable(d):
                        found.append(Path(d))
                    continue
                nxt += [e.path for e in entries if e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False)]
            if left < 0:
                break
            level = nxt
    return found


def _toolchain_pins(projects: Path, budget: int = 2000) -> set[str]:
    """Toolchains a checkout pins (rust-toolchain.toml `channel`, or a legacy rust-toolchain file), in
    ~/Projects/* and one level into its worktree directories, reading at most `budget` entries."""
    pins: set[str] = set()
    dirs: list[Path] = []
    for c in _children(projects)[:budget]:
        if c.is_dir() and not c.is_symlink():
            dirs.append(c)
            if c.name in WORKTREE_DIRS or c.name.endswith("-worktrees"):
                dirs += [w for w in _children(c)[:budget] if w.is_dir() and not w.is_symlink()]
    for d in dirs[:budget]:
        for f in ("rust-toolchain.toml", "rust-toolchain"):
            text = _head(os.fspath(d / f), 4096).decode(errors="replace")
            if not text:
                continue
            m = re.search(r'^\s*channel\s*=\s*"([^"]+)"', text, re.M)
            if m:
                pins.add(m.group(1))
            elif f == "rust-toolchain" and text.strip() and "\n" not in text.strip() and "[" not in text:
                pins.add(text.strip())
            break
    return pins


def rustup_verdict(path: Path) -> str:
    """Why a rustup toolchain stays whatever its age, or "". Only rustup puts one back, so a toolchain
    goes only from a rustup home (settings.toml beside toolchains/) whose rustup binary exists, and
    never one that something names: the default, a directory override, $RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN, or a
    rust-toolchain file of a checkout under ~/Projects."""
    home = path.parent.parent
    settings = home / "settings.toml"
    if path.parent.name != "toolchains" or not settings.is_file():
        return "a toolchain outside a rustup home"
    cargo_home = Path(os.environ.get("CARGO_HOME") or HOME / ".cargo")
    if not (shutil.which("rustup") or (cargo_home / "bin/rustup").is_file() or (home.parent / ".cargo/bin/rustup").is_file()):
        return "rustup is not installed to reinstall it"
    try:
        text = settings.read_text()
    except OSError:
        return "unreadable rustup settings"
    pinned = set()
    for m in re.finditer(r'^\s*default_toolchain\s*=\s*"([^"]+)"', text, re.M):
        pinned.add(m.group(1))
    overrides = text.partition("[overrides]")[2]
    for m in re.finditer(r'=\s*"([^"]+)"', overrides):
        pinned.add(m.group(1))
    if os.environ.get("RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN"):
        pinned.add(os.environ["RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN"])
    pinned |= _toolchain_pins(HOME / "Projects")
    for tc in pinned:
        if path.name == tc or path.name.startswith(tc + "-"):
            return "a pinned toolchain (default, override, RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN or rust-toolchain file)"
    return ""


def keep_verdict(fam: "Family", path: Path) -> str:
    """Why a candidate stays whatever its age, or ""."""
    if fam.id == "cmux-seeds":
        return seed_verdict(path)
    if fam.discover and stat_layout(os.fspath(path)) == "rustup-toolchain":
        return rustup_verdict(path)
    if fam.checkout_builds and path.parent.name == "cache" and path.parent.parent.name == "apple-build":
        return apple_build_verdict(path)
    return ""


def git_state(path: Path, depth: int = 5, budget: int = 20000, sealed: bool = False) -> str:
    """"git" when version-control state (VCS_MARKERS) exists up to `depth` levels down, "none" when the
    whole tree was searched without finding one, "unchecked" when the search could not finish.

    A clone inside a scratch directory can hold unpushed commits, so callers treat "unchecked"
    like "git" (fail closed); so is any read error other than an entry vanishing, and another
    filesystem mounted inside (a deletion would descend into it). Package and build-output directories
    are only checked for version control of their own, not searched. With `sealed` (families whose items
    are caches by where they sit), a sealed cache (stat_layout, listing_layout), at path or anywhere in
    the walk, is checked at its top level and the one below, where a person's clone would sit, and not
    entered further; a hex-sharded store is read in full, but only as blobs, and a directory in a shard
    is walked as usual. Every entry read counts against `budget`, or for sealed caches SEALED_BUDGET.
    """
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return "none"
    except OSError:
        return "unchecked"
    if not stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
        return "none"  # a file (a leaked library in the temp dir) holds no checkout
    dev = st.st_dev
    sealed_left = SEALED_BUDGET

    def mounted(e: os.DirEntry) -> bool:
        try:
            return e.stat(follow_symlinks=False).st_dev != dev
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return False
        except OSError:
            return True

    def listing(d: str, sealed_budget: bool) -> list[os.DirEntry] | str:
        """d's entries, or "git" / "unchecked" / "gone"."""
        nonlocal budget, sealed_left
        out: list[os.DirEntry] = []
        try:
            with os.scandir(d) as it:
                for e in it:
                    if sealed_budget:
                        sealed_left -= 1
                        if sealed_left < 0:
                            return "unchecked"
                    else:
                        budget -= 1
                        if budget < 0:
                            return "unchecked"
                    if e.name in VCS_MARKERS or (e.name == "HEAD" and looks_like_repository(d)):
                        return "git"  # a bare repository holds commits just like a checkout
                    out.append(e)
        except (FileNotFoundError, NotADirectoryError):
            return "gone"  # vanished, or replaced by a file, since it was listed
        except OSError:
            return "unchecked"  # EIO, ESTALE, EACCES: not evidence that nothing is there
        return out

    frontier = [str(path)]
    for _ in range(depth + 1):
        nxt: list[str] = []
        for d in frontier:
            if sealed and stat_layout(d) and not vcs_at(d):
                entries = listing(d, True)
                if isinstance(entries, str):
                    if entries == "gone":
                        continue
                    return entries
                for e in entries:
                    if e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False) and (mounted(e) or vcs_at(e.path)):
                        return "unchecked" if mounted(e) else "git"
                continue
            entries = listing(d, False)
            if isinstance(entries, str):
                if entries == "gone":
                    continue
                return entries
            dirs = [e for e in entries if e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False)]
            if any(mounted(e) for e in dirs):
                return "unchecked"
            layout = listing_layout(d, entries) if sealed else ""
            if layout == "hash-sharded-store":
                # blobs are cheap to list and the shards were only sampled: read every shard (on the
                # sealed budget) and walk anything in one that is neither a blob nor a deeper shard
                stack = [e.path for e in dirs if HEX_SHARD.fullmatch(e.name)]
                dirs = [e for e in dirs if not HEX_SHARD.fullmatch(e.name)]
                while stack:
                    inner = listing(stack.pop(), True)
                    if isinstance(inner, str):
                        if inner == "gone":
                            continue
                        return inner
                    for e in inner:
                        if e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):
                            if mounted(e):
                                return "unchecked"
                            if HEX_SHARD.fullmatch(e.name):
                                stack.append(e.path)
                            else:
                                dirs.append(e)
            elif layout:
                # sealed: its entries were just read; a clone one level below its top is the last look
                if any(vcs_at(e.path) for e in dirs):
                    return "git"
                continue
            for e in dirs:
                if e.name in REPRODUCIBLE_DIRS:
                    if vcs_at(e.path):
                        return "git"  # e.g. `git clone x target`; not searched deeper
                else:
                    nxt.append(e.path)
        frontier = nxt
    return "unchecked" if frontier else "none"


def looks_like_repository(d: str) -> bool:
    """A bare repository or a copied git dir: no .git entry of its own, but HEAD, objects and refs."""
    return (os.path.isfile(os.path.join(d, "HEAD")) and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(d, "objects"))
            and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(d, "refs")))


def checkouts(path: Path, depth: int = 5, budget: int = 20000) -> list[Path] | None:
    """Every checkout (a directory holding .git) or bare repository up to `depth` levels below
    path, not searching inside one; None when the search could not finish."""
    found: list[Path] = []
    frontier = [str(path)]
    for _ in range(depth + 1):
        nxt: list[str] = []
        for d in frontier:
            if os.path.lexists(os.path.join(d, ".git")) or looks_like_repository(d):
                found.append(Path(d))
                continue
            try:
                with os.scandir(d) as it:
                    for e in it:
                        budget -= 1
                        if budget < 0:
                            return None
                        if e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):
                            if e.name not in REPRODUCIBLE_DIRS:
                                nxt.append(e.path)
                            elif os.path.lexists(os.path.join(e.path, ".git")) or looks_like_repository(e.path):
                                found.append(Path(e.path))  # as git_state: not searched deeper
            except PermissionError:
                return None
            except OSError:
                pass
        frontier = nxt
    return None if frontier else found


_NESTED_WALKS: dict[str, list[Path] | None] = {}  # one walk per item per survey or apply pass


def nested_checkouts(path: Path) -> list[Path] | None:
    key = str(path)
    if key not in _NESTED_WALKS:
        _NESTED_WALKS[key] = checkouts(path, NESTED_DEPTH, NESTED_BUDGET)
    return _NESTED_WALKS[key]


def family_git_state(fam: "Family", path: Path) -> str:
    """git_state, or for a nested_git family the full-depth checkout search."""
    if not fam.nested_git:
        # a family whose items are caches by where they sit may skip their sealed parts; one that deletes
        # disposable checkouts (tmp, scratch) searches everything
        return git_state(path, sealed=not fam.git_disposable)
    roots = nested_checkouts(path)
    return "unchecked" if roots is None else "git" if roots else "none"


def bare_loss(gitdir: Path) -> str:
    """repo_loss for a bare repository or copied git dir, and for every submodule repository
    kept under its modules/ (they are deleted with it)."""
    if (loss := repo_loss(gitdir)):
        return loss
    modules = gitdir / "modules"
    if not modules.is_dir() or modules.is_symlink():
        return ""
    unreadable: list[str] = []
    for dirpath, dirnames, _ in os.walk(modules, onerror=lambda e: unreadable.append(str(e.filename))):
        if unreadable:
            return "unreadable submodule store"
        if looks_like_repository(dirpath):
            if (loss := repo_loss(Path(dirpath))):
                return f"{os.path.relpath(dirpath, gitdir)}: {loss}"
            dirnames[:] = ["modules"] if "modules" in dirnames else []  # nested submodules only
    return "unreadable submodule store" if unreadable else ""


def _load_keep() -> dict:
    try:
        keep = json.loads(NESTED_KEEP.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    return {k: v for k, v in keep.items() if isinstance(v, dict) and isinstance(v.get("at"), (int, float))} \
        if isinstance(keep, dict) else {}


def family_git_loss(fam: "Family", path: Path) -> str:
    """git_loss for the checkout at path, or with fam.nested_git for every checkout inside it."""
    if not fam.nested_git:
        return git_loss(path)
    keep = _load_keep()
    hit = keep.get(str(path))
    if isinstance(hit, dict) and time.time() - hit.get("at", 0) < NESTED_KEEP_S and hit.get("loss"):
        return str(hit["loss"])
    loss = _nested_loss(path)
    if loss:
        keep = {k: v for k, v in keep.items()
                if isinstance(v, dict) and time.time() - v.get("at", 0) < NESTED_KEEP_S}
        keep[str(path)] = {"at": time.time(), "loss": loss}
        try:
            NESTED_KEEP.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            tmp = NESTED_KEEP.with_suffix(f".{os.getpid()}.tmp")
            tmp.write_text(json.dumps(keep))
            os.replace(tmp, NESTED_KEEP)
        except OSError:
            pass
    return loss


def ignored_checkout_loss(root: Path) -> str:
    """checkouts() stops at a checkout, and its status hides ignored directories, so a clone kept
    in one (vendor/ in .gitignore) would go unjudged. Build-output directories are skipped as
    everywhere else: SwiftPM and friends keep reproducible dependency clones there."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(root), "status", "--porcelain", "-z", "--ignored=matching",
                            "--untracked-files=normal"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60,
                           env={**os.environ, "GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS": "0"})
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return "unreadable ignored files"
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return "unreadable ignored files"
    for entry in r.stdout.split("\0"):  # -z: paths unquoted, so none is misread and skipped
        if not entry.startswith("!! ") or not entry.endswith("/"):
            continue
        rel = entry[3:].rstrip("/")
        if Path(rel).name in REPRODUCIBLE_DIRS:
            continue
        inner = checkouts(root / rel, NESTED_DEPTH, NESTED_BUDGET)
        if inner is None:
            return f"{rel}: too deep or large to find every checkout"
        for sub in inner:
            if not os.path.lexists(sub / ".git"):
                if (loss := bare_loss(sub)):
                    return f"{sub.relative_to(root)}: {loss}"
            elif (loss := git_loss(sub)) or (loss := ignored_checkout_loss(sub)):
                return f"{sub.relative_to(root)}: {loss}"
    return ""


def _nested_loss(path: Path) -> str:
    roots = nested_checkouts(path)
    if roots is None:
        return "too deep or large to find every checkout"
    inside = os.path.realpath(path) + "/"
    for root in roots:
        where = root.relative_to(path)
        if not os.path.lexists(root / ".git"):
            if (loss := bare_loss(root)):
                return f"{where}: {loss}"
            continue
        if (loss := git_loss(root)):
            return f"{where}: {loss}"
        if (loss := ignored_checkout_loss(root)):
            return f"{where}: {loss}"
        # git_loss keeps a linked worktree because its repository stays; here the repository
        # may be inside the item being deleted
        if (root / ".git").is_file():
            common = git_common_dir(root)
            if not common or (os.path.realpath(common) + "/").startswith(inside):
                return f"{where}: worktree of a repository inside this item"
    return ""


def has_git(path: Path) -> bool:
    return git_state(path) != "none"


def git_loss(path: Path) -> str:
    """"" when deleting this checkout loses nothing git holds, else what it would lose.

    Only a checkout rooted at path is judged; any failure or doubt counts as a loss.
    """
    dotgit = path / ".git"
    if not os.path.lexists(dotgit) or dotgit.is_symlink():
        return "checkout below the top level"

    def git(*args: str) -> str | None:
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(path), *args], capture_output=True, text=True,
                               timeout=60, env={**os.environ, "GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS": "0"})
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            return None
        return r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 else None

    top = git("rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if top is None or os.path.realpath(top.strip()) != os.path.realpath(path):
        return "not a readable checkout"
    # --ignore-submodules=none overrides submodule.*.ignore, so dirty submodules count
    status = git("status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=all", "--ignore-submodules=none")
    if status is None or status.strip():
        return "uncommitted or untracked changes"
    # status hides edits to files flagged assume-unchanged (lowercase tag) or skip-worktree (S)
    flags = git("ls-files", "-v")
    if flags is None or any(ln[:1].islower() or ln[:1] == "S" for ln in flags.splitlines()):
        return "files hidden from status"
    own = git("rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-dir")
    if own is None:
        return "not a readable checkout"
    # submodule repositories live in this checkout's git dir (a linked worktree's own one),
    # so they are deleted with it
    if (loss := submodule_loss(path, Path(own.strip()))):
        return loss
    if dotgit.is_dir():
        wts = git("worktree", "list", "--porcelain")
        if wts is None or sum(ln.startswith("worktree ") for ln in wts.splitlines()) != 1:
            return "other worktrees use this repository"
        if (loss := repo_loss(dotgit)):
            return loss
    else:  # linked worktree: branches and stash live in the repository, which stays
        common = git("rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")
        if common is None or os.path.realpath(common.strip()).startswith(os.path.realpath(path) + "/"):
            return "repository inside the worktree"
        if os.path.lexists(os.path.join(own.strip(), "locked")):
            return "locked worktree"
        # refs/bisect and refs/worktree belong to the worktree and go with it
        refs = git("for-each-ref", "--count=1", "--contains", "HEAD", "--format=%(refname)",
                   "refs/heads", "refs/tags", "refs/remotes")
        if refs is None or not refs.strip():
            return "HEAD on no ref"
        # so does its HEAD reflog: a commit made here and then checked away from lives only there
        tips = local_tips(Path(own.strip()), worktree_only=True)
        if tips is None:
            return "unreadable worktree reflog"
        left = git("rev-list", "-n1", *tips, "--not", "--branches", "--tags", "--remotes") \
            if tips else ""
        if left is None or left.strip():
            return "commits only this worktree's reflog holds"
    return ""


def _git_dir_run(gitdir: Path, *args: str, timeout: int = 60,
                 network: bool = False) -> str | None:
    env = {**os.environ, "GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS": "0", "GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT": "0"}
    if network and "GIT_SSH_COMMAND" not in env:
        env["GIT_SSH_COMMAND"] = "ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=10"
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "--git-dir", str(gitdir), *args], capture_output=True,
                           text=True, timeout=timeout, env=env)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    return r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 else None


ZERO = re.compile(r"0{40}(0{24})?$")


# refs a linked worktree keeps for itself; they are deleted with it
WORKTREE_REFS = ("refs/bisect", "refs/worktree", "refs/rewritten")


def local_tips(gitdir: Path, worktree_only: bool = False) -> list[str] | None:
    """Every commit this repository points at on its own: HEAD, all refs but remote-tracking
    ones, and every reflog entry (a commit left behind by `submodule update` lives only there).
    With worktree_only, only what a linked worktree's git dir holds for itself: HEAD, its
    per-worktree refs and its reflogs. None when they cannot all be read."""
    refs = _git_dir_run(gitdir, "for-each-ref",
                        "--format=%(refname)%09%(objectname)%09%(*objectname)",
                        *(WORKTREE_REFS if worktree_only else ()))
    head = _git_dir_run(gitdir, "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", "HEAD")
    if refs is None or head is None:
        return None
    tips = {head.strip()}
    for line in refs.splitlines():
        name, obj, peeled = (line.split("\t") + ["", ""])[:3]
        if not name.startswith("refs/remotes/"):
            tips.add(peeled or obj)
    logs = gitdir / "logs"
    if logs.is_dir():
        for f in logs.rglob("*"):
            if f.is_file() and not f.relative_to(logs).parts[:2] == ("refs", "remotes"):
                try:
                    lines = f.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines()
                except OSError:
                    return None
                tips.update(ln.split(" ", 2)[1] for ln in lines if ln.count(" ") >= 2)
    return sorted(t for t in tips if t and not ZERO.match(t))


NETWORK_URL = re.compile(r"(https?|ssh|git|git\+ssh|ssh\+git)://|[^/.][^/:]*:(?!//)")


TRUSTED_LOCAL_REMOTES: tuple[str, ...] = ()  # tests: local repos standing in for a server


def network_remotes(gitdir: Path) -> list[str] | None:
    """Remotes on another machine. A local path or file:// remote can be one more scratch repo
    about to be deleted too (two clones fetching from each other vouch for commits that exist
    nowhere else), so it never counts as a copy. None when the config cannot be read."""
    out = _git_dir_run(gitdir, "config", "--get-regexp", r"^remote\..*\.url$")
    if out is None:
        return [] if _git_dir_run(gitdir, "rev-parse", "--git-dir") is not None else None
    names = set()
    for line in out.splitlines():
        key, _, url = line.partition(" ")
        name = key[len("remote."):-len(".url")]
        trusted = any(url == t or url.startswith(t + "/") for t in TRUSTED_LOCAL_REMOTES)
        if trusted or (NETWORK_URL.match(url) and not url.startswith("file:")):
            names.add(name)
        else:
            names.discard(name)  # a remote with any local URL is not trusted
            names.add(f"\0{name}")
    return sorted(n for n in names if not n.startswith("\0") and f"\0{n}" not in names)


def remote_has(gitdir: Path, commits: list[str]) -> bool:
    """True when some remote of this repository confirms it holds every one of `commits`.

    `fetch --negotiate-only` asks the server which of these it has in common and transfers no
    objects and writes no refs. An unreachable server, a refusal or a partial answer is False.
    """
    remotes = network_remotes(gitdir)
    if not remotes:
        return False
    missing = set(commits)
    for remote in remotes:
        acked = _git_dir_run(gitdir, "-c", "protocol.version=2", "fetch", "--negotiate-only",
                             *[f"--negotiation-tip={c}" for c in sorted(missing)], remote,
                             network=True)
        if acked is not None:
            missing -= set(acked.split())
        if not missing:
            return True
    return False


def repo_loss(gitdir: Path) -> str:
    """"" when deleting this (submodule) repository loses nothing: no stash, no worktrees of
    its own, and every local commit on a remote-tracking ref or confirmed by a remote."""
    if (gitdir / "worktrees").is_dir() and any((gitdir / "worktrees").iterdir()):
        return "has worktrees of its own"
    stash = _git_dir_run(gitdir, "for-each-ref", "--count=1", "refs/stash")
    if stash is None or stash.strip():
        return "stash entries"
    tips = local_tips(gitdir)
    if tips is None:
        return "unreadable refs"
    if not tips:
        return ""
    remotes = network_remotes(gitdir)
    if remotes is None:
        return "unreadable remotes"
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "--git-dir", str(gitdir), "rev-list", "--max-count=5001",
                            "--stdin", "--not", *[f"--remotes={n}/*" for n in remotes]],
                           input="\n".join(tips) + "\n",
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60,
                           env={**os.environ, "GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS": "0"})
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return "unreadable history"
    if r.returncode != 0 or len(r.stdout.split()) > 5000:
        return "unreadable history"
    outside = set(r.stdout.split())
    unconfirmed = [t for t in tips if t in outside]  # every outside commit is below one of these
    if not unconfirmed:
        return ""
    if len(unconfirmed) > 64 or not remote_has(gitdir, unconfirmed):
        return "commits no remote confirms"
    return ""


def submodule_loss(worktree: Path, gitdir: Path, seen: set[str] | None = None,
                   depth: int = 0) -> str:
    """"" when every submodule repository this checkout would take with it loses nothing.

    Those are the repositories stored under its git dir (`modules/`, nested ones included) and
    any embedded in its tree (a submodule whose `.git` is a directory), recursively.
    """
    seen = set() if seen is None else seen
    if depth > 8 or len(seen) > 200:
        return "submodules nested too deep to check"
    repos: list[tuple[str, Path]] = []
    frontier = [gitdir / "modules"] if (gitdir / "modules").exists() else []
    while frontier:
        d = frontier.pop()
        if d.is_symlink() or not d.is_dir():
            return "unreadable submodule store"
        for c in _children(d):
            if c.is_symlink() or not c.is_dir():
                continue
            if (c / "HEAD").is_file() and (c / "objects").is_dir():
                repos.append((os.path.relpath(c, gitdir / "modules"), c))
                if (c / "modules").exists():
                    frontier.append(c / "modules")
            else:
                frontier.append(c)  # a path component of a nested submodule name
    links = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(worktree), "ls-files", "-s"], capture_output=True,
                           text=True, timeout=60, env={**os.environ, "GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS": "0"})
    if links.returncode != 0:
        return "unreadable index"
    subs = [worktree / ln.split("\t", 1)[1] for ln in links.stdout.splitlines()
            if ln.startswith("160000 ")]
    for sub in subs:
        dotgit = sub / ".git"
        if dotgit.is_dir() and not dotgit.is_symlink():
            repos.append((os.path.relpath(sub, worktree), dotgit))
    for name, repo in repos:
        key = os.path.realpath(repo)
        if key in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(key)
        if (loss := repo_loss(repo)):
            return f"submodule {name}: {loss}"
    for sub in subs:  # submodules of submodules, wherever their repositories live
        if not os.path.lexists(sub / ".git"):
            continue  # not initialized: nothing of it is here
        own = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(sub), "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute",
                              "--git-dir"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        if own.returncode != 0:
            return f"submodule {os.path.relpath(sub, worktree)}: not readable"
        if (loss := submodule_loss(sub, Path(own.stdout.strip()), seen, depth + 1)):
            return loss
    return ""


def git_common_dir(path: Path) -> str:
    """The repository of a linked worktree, so its record can be pruned after deletion."""
    if not (path / ".git").is_file():
        return ""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(path), "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute",
                            "--git-common-dir"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return ""  # callers treat an unknown repository as one to keep
    return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else ""


UNREADABLE: set[str] = set()


def _entries(path: Path) -> list[os.DirEntry]:
    """Like _children, but entries carry their type, so filtering tens of thousands (a macOS
    $TMPDIR) costs no stat per entry."""
    try:
        with os.scandir(path) as it:
            return list(it)
    except PermissionError:
        UNREADABLE.add(str(path))
        return []
    except OSError:
        return []


def _children(path: Path) -> list[Path]:
    try:
        return list(path.iterdir())
    except PermissionError:
        UNREADABLE.add(str(path))
        return []
    except OSError:
        return []


def owner_retired(fam: Family) -> bool:
    """True when the family names its owner's launchd service and that service is not loaded."""
    if not fam.owner_job or not DARWIN:
        return False
    try:
        # 113 is launchctl's "could not find service"; any other failure is not evidence of retirement
        return subprocess.run(["launchctl", "print", fam.owner_job], capture_output=True,
                              timeout=10).returncode == 113
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return False  # unknown: say nothing rather than call a live owner retired


def _pid_alive(pid: int) -> bool:
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        return False
    except PermissionError:
        return True  # someone else's live process
    return True


def cmux_active(path: Path, levels: int = 3, budget: int = 5000) -> bool:
    """cmuxterm-hq's reload-cloud marks a slot busy while a job runs: <slot>/.cmux-active/pid with a
    live pid, or work/<slot>/.lock (lib/cloud-slot-runtime.sh). Either, at or up to `levels` below path,
    means in use. An unreadable pid, or a tree too big to search within `budget`, counts as busy."""
    level = [path]
    for depth in range(levels + 1):
        nxt: list[Path] = []
        for d in level:
            if d.parent.name == "work" and (d / ".lock").exists():  # hq's slot lock, not any tool's .lock
                return True
            marker = d / ".cmux-active" / "pid"
            if marker.exists():
                try:
                    if _pid_alive(int(marker.read_text().split()[0])):
                        return True
                except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
                    return True
            if depth == levels:
                continue
            for c in _children(d):
                budget -= 1
                if budget < 0:
                    return True
                if c.name != ".cmux-active" and not c.is_symlink() and c.is_dir():
                    nxt.append(c)
        level = nxt
    return False


def job_busy(fam: Family, path: Path) -> bool:
    """A cmux job unit's slots are marked in work/<slot>, not beside DerivedData/<slot>, so a
    candidate in the job cache is busy when any slot of its whole unit (path.parent) is."""
    if fam.id == "cmux-job-cache":
        return cmux_active(path.parent)
    # a sealed cache (home-caches) is never a job unit, and cmux_active calls a tree larger than its
    # budget busy, which would keep bun's install cache forever. Nor is cmux CI's warm state: a DerivedData
    # there holds thousands of module cache directories within three levels, so every seed read as busy and
    # none was ever evicted (cmux15, 2026-09-28). A job using one is seen by its command line and cwd.
    if fam.id in FLEET_FAMILY_IDS:
        return False
    if fam.id == SPM_SCRATCH:  # dozens of package builds within three levels; its lock is its in-use signal
        return False
    return fam.reclaimable and not fam.discover and cmux_active(path)


def value(item: "Item", fam: Family) -> float:
    """Deletion order under pressure: space freed per minute of rebuild, favouring what has sat idle
    longest (a tree untouched for days is less likely to be read again)."""
    jobs = 0 if item.jobs_since is None else item.jobs_since / max(POLICY["pressure_jobs"], 1)
    return item.bytes * (1 + item.idle_hours / 24 + jobs) / max(fam.rebuild_minutes, 0.1)


def holds_socket(path: Path, levels: int = 2, budget: int = 3000) -> bool:
    """True when a Unix socket sits up to `levels` below path (tmux, ssh-agent, X11, IDE servers).

    /proc shows a socket a process holds as socket:[inode], not as a path, so neither the open
    file nor the command line check sees it. A directory holding one is treated as in use.
    """
    frontier = [str(path)]
    for _ in range(levels):
        nxt: list[str] = []
        for d in frontier:
            try:
                with os.scandir(d) as it:
                    for e in it:
                        budget -= 1
                        if budget < 0:
                            return False
                        try:
                            st = e.stat(follow_symlinks=False)
                        except OSError:
                            continue
                        if stat.S_ISSOCK(st.st_mode):
                            return True
                        if stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
                            nxt.append(e.path)
            except OSError:
                pass
        frontier = nxt
    return False


SEARCH_SKIP = REPRODUCIBLE_DIRS | {".git"}


def _search(fam: Family, budget: int = 20000) -> list[Path]:
    found: list[Path] = []
    level = [fam.root]
    for _ in range(fam.depth):
        nxt: list[Path] = []
        for p in level:
            for c in _children(p):
                budget -= 1
                if budget < 0:
                    return found
                if c.name in fam.skip or c.is_symlink() or not c.is_dir():
                    continue
                if c.name == fam.match and (not fam.marker or (c / fam.marker).exists()):
                    found.append(c)
                elif c.name not in SEARCH_SKIP:
                    nxt.append(c)
        level = nxt
    return found


_DISCOVERED: dict[str, list[Path]] = {}


def candidates(fam: Family) -> list[Path]:
    if fam.discover:
        key = os.fspath(fam.root)
        if key not in _DISCOVERED:  # one walk per run: main() and survey both ask
            _DISCOVERED[key] = discover_caches(fam.root)
        level = list(_DISCOVERED[key])
    elif fam.checkout_builds:
        key = "checkout-builds:" + os.fspath(fam.root)
        if key not in _DISCOVERED:
            _DISCOVERED[key] = checkout_builds(fam.root)
        level = list(_DISCOVERED[key])
    elif fam.match:
        level = _search(fam)
    else:
        level = [fam.root]
        for _ in range(fam.depth):
            level = [Path(e.path) for p in level for e in _entries(p)
                     if e.name not in fam.skip
                     and not (fam.skip_prefixes and e.name.startswith(fam.skip_prefixes))
                     and (e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False)
                          or (fam.files and e.is_file(follow_symlinks=False)))]
        if fam.include:
            level = [p for p in level if p.name.startswith(fam.include)]
    uid = os.getuid()
    owned = []
    for p in level:
        if never_delete(p):
            continue
        try:
            if p.lstat().st_uid == uid:
                owned.append(p)
        except OSError:
            pass  # vanished mid-scan
    return owned


def fresh_for_apply(known: dict[str, int], fams: list[Family]) -> dict[str, int]:
    """The snapshot sizes an --apply may reuse: sizes decide what gets deleted under pressure, so every
    reclaimable candidate is re-measured. A discovering family's root is the whole home directory, so
    only its current candidates are dropped, not every size below it (that re-measured the report-only
    checkouts too and ran past the hooks' timeout on the minis)."""
    drop = {os.fspath(p) for f in fams if f.reclaimable and (f.discover or f.checkout_builds)
            for p in candidates(f)}

    def candidate_of_selected(k: str) -> bool:
        if k in drop:
            return True
        for f in fams:
            if not f.reclaimable or f.discover or f.checkout_builds:
                continue
            root = str(f.root) + "/"
            if k.startswith(root) and k[len(root):].split("/")[0] not in f.skip:
                return True
        return False
    return {k: v for k, v in known.items() if not candidate_of_selected(k)}


def load_snapshot_meta(path: Path = SNAPSHOT) -> tuple[float, dict[str, int], dict[str, float]]:
    try:
        doc = json.loads(path.read_text())
        sizes = {k: int(v) for k, v in doc["sizes"].items()}
        at = float(doc["at"])
        measured = doc.get("measured_at") if isinstance(doc.get("measured_at"), dict) else {}
        times = {k: float(v) for k, v in measured.items() if k in sizes and isinstance(v, (int, float))}
        return at, sizes, times
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
        return 0.0, {}, {}


def load_snapshot(path: Path = SNAPSHOT) -> tuple[float, dict[str, int]]:
    at, sizes, _ = load_snapshot_meta(path)
    return at, sizes


def save_snapshot(sizes: dict[str, int], path: Path = SNAPSHOT, at: float | None = None,
                  measured_at: dict[str, float] | None = None) -> None:
    """`at` is when the oldest size was measured; per-item times prevent stale reports starving refresh."""
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path.with_suffix(f".{os.getpid()}.tmp")
    payload = {"at": time.time() if at is None else at, "sizes": sizes}
    if measured_at is not None:
        payload["measured_at"] = {k: v for k, v in measured_at.items() if k in sizes}
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(payload))
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def _fresh(path: Path, seconds: float) -> bool:
    try:
        return time.time() - path.stat().st_mtime < seconds
    except OSError:
        return False


def survey(families: list[Family], idle_hours: float, min_bytes: int,
           known: dict[str, int] | None = None, defer_bulk: bool = False) -> list[Item]:
    """Judge every candidate live. Sizes come from `known` when present; the rest are measured.

    With defer_bulk, unsized candidates of bulk_sizes families are left out instead of measured,
    so a report stays fast; the background refresh sizes them for the next one.
    """
    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=1) as bg:
        evidence = bg.submit(process_evidence)  # lsof is the slowest single call; overlap it
        return _survey(families, idle_hours, min_bytes, known, evidence, defer_bulk)


def _survey(families, idle_hours, min_bytes, known, evidence, defer_bulk=False) -> list[Item]:
    _NESTED_WALKS.clear()
    now = time.time()
    items: list[Item] = []
    known = known if known is not None else {}
    work = [(fam, path) for fam in families for path in candidates(fam)]
    if defer_bulk:
        work = [(fam, path) for fam, path in work if not fam.bulk_sizes or str(path) in known]
    for fam in families:
        # a few new entries are cheaper to size one by one than by re-walking the whole root
        if fam.bulk_sizes and sum(str(p) not in known for f, p in work if f is fam) >= BULK_SIZE_MIN:
            known.update({k: v for k, v in du_children(fam.root).items() if k not in known})
    missing = [path for _, path in work if str(path) not in known]
    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=8) as pool:
        measured = dict(zip(map(str, missing), pool.map(du_bytes, missing)))
    known.update(measured)
    sizes = [known[str(path)] for _, path in work]
    floors = [min_bytes if fam.min_bytes is None else fam.min_bytes for fam, _ in work]
    idles = [(now - item_mtime(fam, path)) / 3600
             if size >= floor else 0.0 for (fam, path), size, floor in zip(work, sizes, floors)]
    try:
        cwds, cmds = evidence.result()
        blind = ""
    except NoEvidence as e:
        cwds, cmds, blind = [], "", f"no process evidence ({e})"
    opened = open_index(cwds)
    archived = archived_seeds() if any(fam.id == "cmux-seeds" for fam, _ in work) else frozenset()
    for (fam, path), size, idle, floor in zip(work, sizes, idles, floors):
        if size < floor:
            continue
        sp = str(path)
        reasons = []
        if blind:
            reasons.append(blind)
        if in_cwd(sp, opened):
            reasons.append("open by a process")
        if named_by(sp, cmds):
            reasons.append("named by a running command")
        if fam.id == "claude-scratchpad" and claude_session_active(path.name, idle_hours * 3600):
            reasons.append("claude session active")
        if fam.id == SPM_SCRATCH and spm_scratch_held(path):
            reasons.append("a job holds its lock (owned_spm_scratch.py)")
        # The tree walks below cost seconds each on a loaded host, and a recent item is kept anyway,
        # so only an item old enough to delete pays for them (apply re-checks before deleting).
        window = item_window(fam, path, idle_hours, archived)
        keep = keep_verdict(fam, path)
        due, jobs = item_due(fam, idle, window)
        if jobs is not None:
            reasons_jobs = f"{jobs} host jobs since its last use"
        deletable_age = fam.reclaimable and due
        if deletable_age and holds_socket(path):
            reasons.append("holds a socket")
        if deletable_age and job_busy(fam, path):
            reasons.append("a cmux reload job holds its unit (.cmux-active or .lock)")
        if not fam.reclaimable:
            verdict = "report-only"
        elif reasons:
            verdict = "in-use"
        elif fam.retired_only and not owner_retired(fam):
            verdict = "report-only"
        elif keep:
            verdict = "kept"
            reasons.append(keep)
        elif reasons:
            verdict = "in-use"
        elif (not derived_data(fam, path) and (g := family_git_state(fam, path)) != "none"
              and not (g == "git" and fam.git_disposable and due
                       and not (loss := family_git_loss(fam, path)))):
            verdict = "git-checkout" if g == "git" else "unchecked"
            if g == "unchecked":
                reasons.append("too deep or large to rule out a git checkout")
            elif fam.git_disposable and due:
                reasons.append(loss)
        elif not due:
            verdict = "recent"
        else:
            verdict = "reclaimable"
            if path.name in archived and fam.id == "cmux-seeds":
                reasons.append("the seed archive holds it")
        if jobs is not None and verdict in ("recent", "reclaimable"):
            reasons.append(reasons_jobs)
        items.append(Item(fam.id, sp, size, round(idle, 1), verdict, reasons, jobs))
    items.sort(key=lambda i: i.bytes, reverse=True)
    return items


def free_bytes(path: Path) -> tuple[int, int]:
    u = shutil.disk_usage(path)
    return u.free, u.total


def space(spec: str, total: int) -> int:
    """A threshold as bytes: "60" is 60 GiB, "15%" is 15% of this filesystem's size, and
    "15%:40-150" is that share held between 40 and 150 GiB. LO never lifts it past twice the
    share, so a small disk (a 30 GiB VM) is not left permanently under pressure."""
    spec = str(spec).strip()
    share, _, bounds = spec.partition(":")
    if not share.endswith("%"):
        if bounds:
            raise ValueError(f"bounds need a percentage: {spec!r}")
        return int(float(share) * GIB)
    n = int(total * float(share[:-1]) / 100)
    if bounds:
        lo, sep, hi = bounds.partition("-")
        if not sep:
            raise ValueError(f"bounds are LOW-HIGH GiB: {spec!r}")
        lo_b, hi_b = int(float(lo) * GIB), int(float(hi) * GIB)
        if lo_b > hi_b:
            raise ValueError(f"bounds are LOW-HIGH GiB: {spec!r}")
        n = min(max(n, min(lo_b, 2 * n)), hi_b)
    return n


# A GitHub Actions job in progress (any runner dir: actions-runner, actions-runner-glaeda-2, ...). An
# auto-updated runner's bin is a symlink to bin.<version>, which ps shows resolved.
CI_WORKER = re.compile(r"/actions-runner[^/\s]*/bin(?:\.[0-9.]+)?/Runner\.Worker(?:\s|$)")


def ci_jobs() -> list[str]:
    """Command lines of running CI jobs; empty when ps fails (apply's own evidence then fails closed)."""
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["ps", "-axww", "-o", "command="], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60).stdout
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return []
    return [line.strip()[:160] for line in out.splitlines() if CI_WORKER.search(line)]


def emergency_bytes(fs: "Fs", emergency: str, floor_gib: float = 0.0) -> int:
    """The emergency floor on fs: --emergency, lifted on HOME's volume to target_margin_gib above a
    runner's disk floor. A busy mini always has a job running, so below this is the only place pressure
    deletes happen; under the gate's floor they would wait until the runners stopped taking jobs
    (cmux9s, 2026-09-27: every run deferred at 71 GiB free, emergency 46 GiB, floor 50 GiB)."""
    n = space(emergency, fs.total)
    if floor_gib > 0 and not fs.tmpfs and fs.dev == HOME.stat().st_dev:
        n = max(n, min(int((floor_gib + POLICY["target_margin_gib"]) * GIB), fs.total // 2))
    return n


def ci_defer(fss: dict[int, Fs], jobs: list[str], emergency: str, floor_gib: float = 0.0) -> str:
    """Why a pressure run should wait for a running CI job, or "" to go ahead. A bulk delete competes
    with the job's compile for disk and CPU (a 30 GiB delete starved one on a build mini), so it waits
    unless some filesystem under pressure is below the emergency floor (emergency_bytes)."""
    if not jobs:
        return ""
    if any(fs.under and fs.free < emergency_bytes(fs, emergency, floor_gib) for fs in fss.values()):
        return ""
    return f"deferred: a CI job is running ({jobs[0]}); pressure deletes wait unless free drops below {emergency} or {POLICY['target_margin_gib']:g} GiB above a runner floor"


def mount_point(path: Path) -> Path:
    path = Path(os.path.realpath(path))
    dev = path.stat().st_dev
    while path.parent != path and path.parent.stat().st_dev == dev:
        path = path.parent
    return path


@dataclass
class Fs:
    dev: int
    mount: str
    free: int
    total: int
    low: int
    target: int
    tmpfs: bool = False

    @property
    def under(self) -> bool:
        return self.free < self.low


# A glaeda runner's listener gate holds the runner while free disk is under its job-started hook's --min-free-gib,
# and resumes only GATE_DISK_RESUME_GIB (2 GiB) above it. Pressure on a disk must start by then, or a 460 GiB mini
# between the default low (25%:60-200, about 115 GiB) and a 150 GiB floor stays held with nothing freed. The
# target sits target_margin_gib higher, so one run lifts the mini well clear of the floor.
RUNNER_HOOKS = "actions-runner*/glaeda-hooks/job-started.sh"  # glaeda-cmux-runner's default --runner-dir
MIN_FREE_GIB_RE = r"\s--min-free-gib[\s=]+([0-9.eE+-]+)(?=\s|$)"  # the hook's min_free_gib reads the same


def runner_floor_gib(home: Path = HOME) -> float:
    """The highest --min-free-gib among this user's glaeda runner hooks (the last one in each), or 0."""
    floor = 0.0
    for hook in home.glob(RUNNER_HOOKS):
        try:
            values = re.findall(MIN_FREE_GIB_RE, hook.read_text())
            value = float(values[-1]) if values else 0.0
            if math.isfinite(value):
                floor = max(floor, value)
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError):
            continue
    return floor


def filesystems(fams: list[Family], low: str, target: str,
                tmpfs_low: str = "75%", tmpfs_target: str = "90%", floor_gib: float = 0.0) -> dict[int, Fs]:
    out: dict[int, Fs] = {}
    for f in fams:
        dev = f.root.stat().st_dev
        if dev not in out:
            free, total = free_bytes(f.root)
            tmpfs = not DARWIN and fs_type(f.root) == "tmpfs"
            lo, tg = (tmpfs_low, tmpfs_target) if tmpfs else (low, target)
            lo_b, tg_b = space(lo, total), space(tg, total)
            # a runner's disk floor (runner_floor_gib) lifts both on HOME's volume, where the gate measures it,
            # never past half the disk, as space() keeps a small disk from permanent pressure
            if floor_gib > 0 and not tmpfs and dev == HOME.stat().st_dev:
                lo_b = max(lo_b, min(int((floor_gib + POLICY["low_margin_gib"]) * GIB), total // 2))
                tg_b = max(tg_b, min(int((floor_gib + POLICY["target_margin_gib"]) * GIB), total // 2))
            out[dev] = Fs(dev, str(mount_point(f.root)), free, total, lo_b, tg_b, tmpfs)
    return out


def gib(n: int) -> str:
    return f"{n / GIB:6.1f}G"


ACCOUNTING_MAX_AGE_S = 6 * 3600
_ACCOUNTING_MEM: dict[tuple[int, str], list[dict[str, Any]]] = {}


def _accounting_cache() -> dict[str, Any]:
    try:
        doc = json.loads(ACCOUNTING.read_text())
        return doc if isinstance(doc, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}


def filesystem_accounting(fss: list[Fs], items: list[Item]) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
    """Show df-used minus catalogued bytes and bounded top-level volume usage.

    The top-level walk is cached for six hours. Item sizes stay the authoritative measured catalog;
    this report deliberately calls out anything the catalog does not own so a zero reclaimable result
    cannot hide a full volume.
    """
    cached = _accounting_cache()
    now = time.time()
    out: list[dict[str, Any]] = []
    cache_records: dict[str, dict[str, Any]] = {}
    for fs in fss:
        key = str(fs.dev)
        hit = cached.get(key) if isinstance(cached.get(key), dict) else None
        mem = _ACCOUNTING_MEM.get((fs.dev, fs.mount))
        if mem is not None:
            top = mem
            top_at = float(hit.get("at", 0)) if hit else 0.0
        elif not DARWIN:
            # Linux test and development hosts do not need a / walk for the macOS mini report.
            top = []
            top_at = now
        elif hit and now - float(hit.get("at", 0)) < ACCOUNTING_MAX_AGE_S and hit.get("mount") == fs.mount:
            top = hit.get("top_level") if isinstance(hit.get("top_level"), list) else []
            top_at = float(hit.get("at", 0))
        else:
            top_map = du_children(Path(fs.mount))
            top = [{"path": p, "bytes": n} for p, n in sorted(top_map.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1])[:32]]
            top_at = now
        _ACCOUNTING_MEM[(fs.dev, fs.mount)] = top
        measured = 0
        catalogued: list[tuple[Path, int]] = []
        for i in items:
            try:
                if Path(i.path).stat().st_dev == fs.dev:
                    catalogued.append((Path(i.path), i.bytes))
            except OSError:
                continue
        # A parent catalog item already includes every nested item. Count only the highest-level path
        # in each overlapping set so the unmeasured figure remains an accounting gap, not an overlap artifact.
        selected: list[Path] = []
        for path, size in sorted(catalogued, key=lambda pair: (len(pair[0].parts), str(pair[0]))):
            if any(path == parent or parent in path.parents for parent in selected):
                continue
            selected.append(path)
            measured += size
        used = max(0, fs.total - fs.free)
        entry = {"dev": fs.dev, "mount": fs.mount, "used": used, "measured": measured,
                 "unmeasured": max(0, used - measured), "top_level": top}
        out.append(entry)
        cache_records[str(fs.dev)] = {**entry, "at": top_at}
    try:
        ACCOUNTING.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        ACCOUNTING.write_text(json.dumps(cache_records))
    except OSError:
        pass
    return out


def write_health_summary(fss: list[Fs], items: list[Item], accounting: list[dict[str, Any]]) -> None:
    """Write the bounded local summary consumed by mini-health and ci-dash's Health row."""
    try:
        HEALTH_SUMMARY.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        payload = {"schema": "glaeda-disk/v1", "at": int(time.time()),
                   "reclaimable": sum(i.bytes for i in items if i.verdict == "reclaimable"),
                   "accounting": [dict(x) for x in accounting],
                   "filesystems": [{"mount": x.mount, "free": x.free, "total": x.total,
                                    "under": x.under} for x in fss]}
        tmp = HEALTH_SUMMARY.with_suffix(f".{os.getpid()}.tmp")
        tmp.write_text(json.dumps(payload, separators=(",", ":")))
        os.replace(tmp, HEALTH_SUMMARY)
    except OSError:
        pass


def report(items: list[Item], fss: list[Fs], limit: int, idle_hours: float,
           notes: dict[str, str] | None = None, fams: dict[str, Family] | None = None,
           accounting: list[dict[str, Any]] | None = None) -> None:
    for fs in fss:
        print(f"free {gib(fs.free).strip()} of {gib(fs.total).strip()} "
              f"({100 * fs.free / max(fs.total, 1):.0f}%) on {fs.mount}, pressure below "
              f"{gib(fs.low).strip()}{' (tmpfs: RAM and swap)' if fs.tmpfs else ''}"
              f"{' (under pressure)' if fs.under else ''}")
    print(f"idle window {idle_hours:g}h\n")
    for a in accounting or []:
        print(f"unmeasured {gib(a['unmeasured']).strip()} on {a['mount']} "
              f"(df used {gib(a['used']).strip()}, measured {gib(a['measured']).strip()})")
    by_fam: dict[str, int] = {}
    for i in items:
        by_fam[i.family] = by_fam.get(i.family, 0) + i.bytes
    print("by family")
    for fam, n in sorted(by_fam.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]):
        r = sum(i.bytes for i in items if i.family == fam and i.verdict == "reclaimable")
        note = f"  ({notes[fam]})" if notes and fam in notes else ""
        f = (fams or {}).get(fam)
        if f and f.owner:
            note += f"  owner: {f.owner}" + (" (RETIRED: its service is not loaded)" if owner_retired(f) else "")
        print(f"  {gib(n)}  {fam:20s}  reclaimable {gib(r).strip()}{note}")
    print(f"\ntop {limit}")
    for i in items[:limit]:
        why = f" ({', '.join(i.reasons)})" if i.reasons else ""
        path = i.path.replace(str(HOME), "~")
        print(f"  {gib(i.bytes)}  {i.verdict:12s} {i.idle_hours:6.1f}h  {path}{why}")
    for u in sorted(UNREADABLE):
        hint = (f"background jobs need Full Disk Access for {os.path.realpath(sys.executable)}"
                if DARWIN else f"not readable by uid {os.getuid()}; its items are skipped")
        print(f"\ncannot read {u}: permission denied ({hint})", file=sys.stderr)
    total_r = sum(i.bytes for i in items if i.verdict == "reclaimable")
    print(f"\nreclaimable now: {gib(total_r).strip()}  ->  glaeda-disk --apply")


def remove(path: Path, after_rename=None) -> None:
    """Delete path. `after_rename` runs once the tree is out of the way, before the long delete."""
    if path.is_symlink():
        raise OSError("refusing symlink")
    if os.path.ismount(path) or path.lstat().st_dev != path.parent.stat().st_dev:
        raise OSError("refusing a mount point")
    if not path.is_dir():
        path.unlink()
        return
    # Move it out of the way first (same directory, so one atomic rename): a job that starts during a
    # minutes-long delete then finds nothing at the path instead of a half-deleted tree it might reuse.
    # A read-only parent makes the rename fail and apply records the item incomplete: nothing is lost.
    doomed = path.with_name(f"{DELETING_PREFIX}{path.name}-{os.getpid()}")
    os.rename(path, doomed)
    if after_rename is not None:
        after_rename()
    shutil.rmtree(doomed, ignore_errors=False, onerror=_chmod_retry)


def _chmod_retry(func, p, _exc):  # read-only files in SwiftPM/Xcode checkouts
    rwx = stat.S_IWUSR | stat.S_IRUSR | stat.S_IXUSR
    parent = os.path.dirname(p)
    if not os.path.islink(parent):
        os.chmod(parent, rwx)
    if not os.path.islink(p):  # never chmod through a symlink to something outside the tree
        os.chmod(p, rwx)
    func(p)


APPLY_LSOF_ITEMS, APPLY_LSOF_S = 8, 20.0  # apply re-takes lsof for discovered items this often


def apply(items: list[Item], families: dict[str, Family], receipt: Path,
          targets: dict[int, int] | None, idle_hours: float, halt=None) -> int:
    """Delete reclaimable items, largest first. With `targets` (filesystem device -> free bytes
    to stop at), only items on those filesystems are touched, each until its own target. With `halt`
    (the idle sweep's host_busy), each deletion first asks it and the run stops once it names a reason."""
    receipt.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    released = 0
    _NESTED_WALKS.clear()  # re-observe every checkout right before deleting
    try:
        cwds, cmds = process_evidence()
    except NoEvidence as e:
        print(f"refusing to delete anything: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 0
    cwds = open_index(cwds)
    lsof_at, lsof_items = time.monotonic(), 0
    archived = archived_seeds() if any(i.family == "cmux-seeds" for i in items) else frozenset()
    # Among pressure hot-tier items, evict greatest reuse distance, then oldest use.
    def order(i):
        if ACTIVITY_MODE == "pressure" and i.family in FLEET_FAMILY_IDS:
            return (1, i.jobs_since if i.jobs_since is not None else -1, i.idle_hours, i.bytes)
        return (0, value(i, families[i.family]), 0, 0)
    for i in sorted(items, key=order, reverse=True):
        if i.verdict != "reclaimable":
            continue
        p = Path(i.path)
        if targets is not None:
            try:
                dev = p.parent.stat().st_dev
            except OSError:
                continue
            if dev not in targets or free_bytes(p.parent)[0] >= targets[dev]:
                continue
        # fresh re-observation right before the destructive step. cmux jobs clone their warm state with
        # `cp -cR SOURCE ...` at any moment and a clone leaves the source's times alone, so those families
        # re-read the command lines per item rather than trusting the ones from the start of a long run.
        if halt is not None and (why := halt()):
            print(f"stopped: {why}")
            break
        if families[i.family].id in FLEET_FAMILY_IDS:
            # Pressure eligibility is intentionally broad, so take fresh cwd/open-file evidence immediately
            # before each rename. A job may start using a candidate after the survey or during a long pass.
            try:
                opened, cmds = process_evidence()
            except NoEvidence as e:
                print(f"refusing to delete the rest: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
                break
            cwds = open_index(opened)
        if families[i.family].build_output and families[i.family].root.is_relative_to(FLEET_CI):
            cmds = command_lines() or cmds
        fresh_layout = ""
        if families[i.family].discover:
            # a discovered cache is deleted only on its own layout, so both are re-read now: the layout,
            # and the open files (a build may have started using it since the survey's lsof)
            # (lsof costs seconds on a loaded mini, so it is re-taken every APPLY_LSOF_ITEMS items or
            # APPLY_LSOF_S seconds, keeping --pressure --apply inside the hooks' timeout; ps is per item)
            fresh_layout = discoverable(i.path)
            lsof_items += 1
            if lsof_items >= APPLY_LSOF_ITEMS or time.monotonic() - lsof_at >= APPLY_LSOF_S:
                try:
                    opened, cmds = process_evidence()
                except NoEvidence as e:
                    print(f"refusing to delete the rest: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
                    break
                cwds = open_index(opened)
                lsof_at, lsof_items = time.monotonic(), 0
            else:
                cmds = command_lines()
                if not cmds:
                    print("refusing to delete the rest: ps failed", file=sys.stderr)
                    break
        if families[i.family].retired_only and not owner_retired(families[i.family]):
            outcome = "changed:owner-loaded"
        elif families[i.family].discover and not fresh_layout:
            outcome = "changed:layout"
        elif (in_cwd(i.path, cwds) or named_by(i.path, cmds) or holds_socket(p) or job_busy(families[i.family], p)
                or (i.family == "claude-scratchpad" and claude_session_active(p.name, idle_hours * 3600))
                or (i.family == SPM_SCRATCH and spm_scratch_held(p))):
            outcome = "changed:in-use"
        elif not item_due(families[i.family],
                          (time.time() - item_mtime(families[i.family], p)) / 3600,
                          item_window(families[i.family], p, idle_hours, archived))[0]:
            outcome = "changed:modified"
        elif keep_verdict(families[i.family], p):
            outcome = "changed:kept"
        elif not derived_data(families[i.family], p) and not families[i.family].git_disposable and family_git_state(families[i.family], p) != "none":
            outcome = "changed:git"  # a clone appeared at the top of the cache since the survey
        elif (families[i.family].git_disposable and p.is_dir() and not derived_data(families[i.family], p)
              and (g := family_git_state(families[i.family], p)) != "none"
              and (g != "git" or family_git_loss(families[i.family], p))):
            outcome = "changed:git"  # the checkout gained something to lose since the survey
        else:
            try:
                common = git_common_dir(p) if families[i.family].git_disposable else ""
                # rustup skips reinstalling a toolchain whose update hash still matches the channel
                # the moment the toolchain is renamed away, so an interrupted delete cannot leave it behind
                stale_hash = (p.parent.parent / "update-hashes" / p.name
                              if fresh_layout == "rustup-toolchain" else None)
                if i.family == SPM_SCRATCH:
                    gone = remove_spm_scratch(p)  # only under its lock: a job may link it right now
                else:
                    remove(p, after_rename=None if stale_hash is None
                           else lambda h=stale_hash: h.unlink(missing_ok=True))
                    gone = True
                outcome = "reclaimed" if gone else "changed:in-use"
                released += i.bytes if gone else 0
                if common:  # drop the deleted worktree's record from its repository
                    subprocess.run(["git", "--git-dir", common, "worktree", "prune"],
                                   capture_output=True, timeout=60)
            except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
                outcome = f"incomplete:{e.__class__.__name__}"
        rec = {"at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z"), "host": platform.node(),
               "outcome": outcome, "rebuild": families[i.family].rebuild, **asdict(i),
               **({"sweep": "idle"} if halt is not None else {})}
        with receipt.open("a") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps(rec) + "\n")
        print(f"  {outcome:16s} {gib(i.bytes)}  {i.path.replace(str(HOME), '~')}")
    print(f"released {gib(released).strip()}; receipt {receipt}")
    return released


def dedupe_roots(derived_data: Path) -> list[Path]:
    roots = [derived_data / sub for sub in DEDUPE_SUBDIRS]
    for d in _children(derived_data):
        if not d.is_symlink():
            roots += [d / sub for sub in DEDUPE_SUBDIRS]
    return [r for r in roots if r.is_dir() and not r.is_symlink()]


def _file_key(st: os.stat_result) -> list[int]:
    return [st.st_size, st.st_mtime_ns, st.st_ino]


def _sha256(path: str) -> str:
    import hashlib
    h = hashlib.sha256()
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        while chunk := f.read(1 << 20):
            h.update(chunk)
    return h.hexdigest()


def clone_over(canonical: str, target: str, expect: list[int],
               canonical_expect: list[int] | None = None) -> bool:
    """Replace target with an APFS clone of canonical, keeping target's mode and times.

    Refuses when target or canonical changed since they were hashed, checked before and
    after the copy, so a file rewritten mid-run can never be cloned over a verified one. A process that has target open keeps
    reading the old inode; the rename only swaps the directory entry.
    """
    st = os.lstat(target)
    if _file_key(st) != expect or not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
        return False
    if canonical_expect is not None and _file_key(os.lstat(canonical)) != canonical_expect:
        return False
    tmp = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(target), f".{os.path.basename(target)}.glaeda-clone-{os.getpid()}")
    try:
        subprocess.run(["/bin/cp", "-c", canonical, tmp], check=True, capture_output=True)
        os.chmod(tmp, stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode))
        os.utime(tmp, ns=(st.st_atime_ns, st.st_mtime_ns))
        if _file_key(os.lstat(target)) != expect:
            return False
        if canonical_expect is not None and _file_key(os.lstat(canonical)) != canonical_expect:
            return False
        os.rename(tmp, target)
        return True
    finally:
        if os.path.lexists(tmp):
            os.unlink(tmp)


def dedupe(roots: list[Path], state_path: Path = DEDUPE_STATE,
           min_bytes: int = DEDUPE_MIN_BYTES, settle_s: float = 0) -> dict[str, int]:
    """Clone-dedupe identical files. Files modified within settle_s are left for a later run."""
    try:
        state: dict[str, dict] = json.loads(state_path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        state = {}
    files: dict[str, list[int]] = {}
    young = 0
    cutoff = time.time() - settle_s
    for root in roots:
        for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):
            dirnames[:] = [d for d in dirnames if not os.path.islink(os.path.join(dirpath, d))]
            for name in filenames:
                if ".glaeda-clone-" in name:
                    continue
                path = os.path.join(dirpath, name)
                try:
                    st = os.lstat(path)
                except OSError:
                    continue
                if stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) and st.st_size >= min_bytes and st.st_nlink == 1:
                    if st.st_mtime > cutoff:
                        young += 1  # still being downloaded or written
                        continue
                    files[path] = _file_key(st)
    by_size: dict[int, list[str]] = {}
    for path, key in files.items():
        by_size.setdefault(key[0], []).append(path)
    new_state: dict[str, dict] = {}
    groups: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
    hashed = 0
    for paths in by_size.values():
        if len(paths) < 2:
            continue
        for path in paths:
            old = state.get(path)
            if old and old.get("key") == files[path]:
                sha, cloned = old["sha"], old.get("cloned", False)
            else:
                try:
                    sha = _sha256(path)
                except OSError:
                    continue
                cloned, hashed = False, hashed + files[path][0]
            new_state[path] = {"key": files[path], "sha": sha, "cloned": cloned}
            groups.setdefault(sha, []).append(path)
    cloned_bytes = refused = 0
    for sha, paths in groups.items():
        if len(paths) < 2:
            continue
        paths.sort(key=lambda p: (not new_state[p]["cloned"], p))
        canonical = paths[0]
        for target in paths[1:]:
            if new_state[target]["cloned"]:
                continue
            try:
                ok = clone_over(canonical, target, files[target], files[canonical])
                if ok:
                    new_state[target] = {"key": _file_key(os.lstat(target)), "sha": sha,
                                         "cloned": True}
            except (OSError, subprocess.CalledProcessError):
                ok = False
                new_state.pop(target, None)  # vanished or unreadable; rehash next run
            if ok:
                cloned_bytes += files[target][0]
            else:
                refused += 1
        new_state[canonical]["cloned"] = True
    state_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = state_path.with_suffix(f".{os.getpid()}.tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(new_state))
    os.replace(tmp, state_path)
    return {"files": len(files), "hashed_bytes": hashed, "cloned_bytes": cloned_bytes,
            "refused": refused, "young": young}


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("--apply", action="store_true", help="delete reclaimable items")
    ap.add_argument("--pressure", action="store_true",
                    help="only act when free space is below --low; stop at --target")
    ap.add_argument("--idle", action="store_true",
                    help="idle sweep: only while no job runs, delete what sat idle past its family's reuse "
                         "window whatever the free space; with --pressure, only when there is no pressure, "
                         "at most hourly")
    # Air Blue sat at 3-7% free for a day with the old 15%/25%: act earlier and free more per run
    ap.add_argument("--low", default=POLICY["low"],
                    help="pressure threshold per filesystem: GiB free, N%% of its size, or "
                         "N%%:LO-HI to hold that share between LO and HI GiB (default %(default)s)")
    ap.add_argument("--target", default=POLICY["target"],
                    help="stop freeing at this much free, same forms (default %(default)s)")
    ap.add_argument("--tmpfs-low", default="75%",
                    help="pressure threshold for a tmpfs, whose files live in RAM and swap "
                         "(default %(default)s free)")
    ap.add_argument("--tmpfs-target", default="90%",
                    help="stop freeing a tmpfs at this much free (default %(default)s)")
    # One cold cmux job can write 10-36 GiB between two 15-minute runs, so the floor sits well above empty.
    ap.add_argument("--emergency", default=POLICY["emergency"],
                    help="with --pressure --apply: delete even while a CI job runs once free space is "
                         "below this, same forms as --low (default %(default)s)")
    ap.add_argument("--floor-gib", type=float, default=0.0,
                    help="a runner disk floor to honour besides the ones read from runner hooks (runner_floor_gib): "
                         "the runner hook passes its own --min-free-gib when it evicts before refusing a job")
    ap.add_argument("--need", type=float, default=None,
                    help="make room for this many GiB before a job; derives thresholds from the policy margins")
    ap.add_argument("--idle-hours", type=float, default=None,
                    help="idle window (default 24h, or 6h under pressure)")
    ap.add_argument("--family", action="append", help="limit to these family ids")
    ap.add_argument("--top", type=int, default=25)
    ap.add_argument("--min-mib", type=int, default=50)
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--dedupe", action="store_true",
                    help="clone-dedupe identical Xcode package and module-cache files, then exit")
    ap.add_argument("--settle-minutes", type=float, default=0,
                    help="with --dedupe: skip files changed this recently, wait and retry (up to 3 passes)")
    ap.add_argument("--refresh", action="store_true", help="re-measure all sizes now")
    ap.add_argument("--max-age", type=float, default=15,
                    help="minutes before the size snapshot is refreshed in the background (default 15)")
    ap.add_argument("--no-snapshot", action="store_true", help=argparse.SUPPRESS)  # tests
    ap.add_argument("--receipt", type=Path,
                    default=HOME / "Projects/recovery/disk-reclaim/receipts.jsonl")
    ap.add_argument("--root-override", action="append", default=[], metavar="ID=PATH",
                    help=argparse.SUPPRESS)  # tests
    a = ap.parse_args(argv)

    if a.need is not None:
        if not math.isfinite(a.need) or a.need <= 0:
            ap.error("--need must be a positive GiB value")
        a.floor_gib = max(a.floor_gib, a.need)
        a.low = f"{a.need + POLICY['low_margin_gib']:g}"
        a.target = f"{a.need + POLICY['target_margin_gib']:g}"

    fams = default_families()
    for ov in a.root_override:
        fid, _, p = ov.partition("=")
        fams = [replace(f, root=Path(p), files=False, min_bytes=None, git_disposable=False)
                if f.id == fid else f for f in fams]
    if a.family:
        fams = [f for f in fams if f.id in a.family]
    if a.dedupe:
        dd = next((f.root for f in fams if f.id == "xcode-derived-data"), None)
        if dd is None or not DARWIN:
            print("nothing to dedupe (APFS clones of Xcode DerivedData are macOS only)")
            return 0
        before, _ = free_bytes(dd)
        started = time.time()
        for attempt in range(3):
            r = dedupe(dedupe_roots(dd), settle_s=a.settle_minutes * 60)
            after, _ = free_bytes(dd)
            print(f"dedupe: {r['files']} files >= 1 MiB, hashed {gib(r['hashed_bytes']).strip()}, "
                  f"cloned {gib(r['cloned_bytes']).strip()}, refused {r['refused']}, "
                  f"still settling {r['young']}, free {gib(before).strip()} -> {gib(after).strip()} "
                  f"in {time.time() - started:.0f}s", flush=True)
            if not r["young"] or attempt == 2:
                break
            time.sleep(a.settle_minutes * 60)
        return 0
    try:
        floor_gib = max(runner_floor_gib(), a.floor_gib if math.isfinite(a.floor_gib) else 0.0)
        fss = filesystems(fams or [Family("home", HOME, False, "")], a.low, a.target,
                          a.tmpfs_low, a.tmpfs_target, floor_gib)
        space(a.emergency, GIB)
    except ValueError:
        ap.error(f"--low/--target/--tmpfs-*/--emergency take GiB or N%: {a.low!r}, {a.target!r}, "
                 f"{a.tmpfs_low!r}, {a.tmpfs_target!r}, {a.emergency!r}")
    # a tmpfs is under pressure at a quarter full, so it shortens the idle window only for
    # pressure runs, which then touch nothing but that filesystem's families
    under = any(fs.under for fs in fss.values() if a.pressure or not fs.tmpfs)
    sweep = a.idle and not (a.pressure and under)
    if a.pressure and not under:
        for fs in fss.values():
            print(f"no pressure: {gib(fs.free).strip()} free >= {gib(fs.low).strip()} on {fs.mount}")
        if not a.idle:
            return 0
        if not idle_due():
            print(f"idle sweep: the last one ran within {IDLE_SWEEP_EVERY_S // 60} min")
            return 0
    global ACTIVITY_MODE, EMERGENCY_DEVS
    ACTIVITY_MODE = "sweep" if sweep else "pressure" if a.pressure else None
    # the idle sweep judges by reuse windows alone; a pressure run or a report sees the emergency floor
    EMERGENCY_DEVS = frozenset() if sweep else emergency_devs(fss, a.emergency, floor_gib)
    busy = host_busy() if sweep else ""
    if sweep:
        print(f"idle sweep: {'host busy, ' + busy if busy else 'host idle'}")
        # a busy host sweeps too (POLICY defer_during_ci false): only items a job uses are held
        if busy and a.apply and POLICY["defer_during_ci"]:
            return 0
        a.pressure = False  # the sweep reports and acts on every filesystem, without targets
        fams = idle_families(fams)
    if a.pressure:
        # only families on a filesystem under pressure are surveyed or touched
        fams = [f for f in fams if fss[f.root.stat().st_dev].under]
        if a.apply and POLICY["defer_during_ci"] and (why := ci_defer(fss, ci_jobs(), a.emergency, floor_gib)):
            print(why)
            return 0
    if a.apply:
        # a second eviction would only race the first for the same items; its caller re-reads free space instead
        EVICT_LOCK.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        evict_lock = EVICT_LOCK.open("a")  # held until this process exits
        try:
            fcntl.flock(evict_lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except BlockingIOError:
            print("another eviction running; skipped")
            return 0
    idle = a.idle_hours if a.idle_hours is not None else IDLE_SWEEP_HOURS if sweep else (6 if under else 24)
    at, known, measured_at = (0.0, {}, {}) if a.refresh or a.no_snapshot else load_snapshot_meta()
    original_known = set(known)
    if a.apply:
        # sizes decide what gets deleted under pressure, so never trust stale candidate sizes
        known = fresh_for_apply(known, fams)
    now = time.time()
    stale_s = a.max_age * 60
    if a.pressure:
        # A pressure decision must not be based on a report-only item measured hours ago.
        known = {k: v for k, v in known.items() if now - measured_at.get(k, at) <= stale_s}
    known = {k: v for k, v in known.items() if os.path.lexists(k)}
    dropped_for_measurement = original_known - set(known)
    known_before = set(known)
    before = len(known)
    measure_lock = None
    try:
        MEASURE_LOCK.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        measure_lock = MEASURE_LOCK.open("a")
        fcntl.flock(measure_lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except BlockingIOError:
        if a.pressure or a.refresh:
            print("another measurement running; skipped")
            return 0
        measure_lock = None
    items = survey(fams, idle, a.min_mib * 1024**2, known, defer_bulk=bool(at) and not a.apply)
    # Release the measurement lock before starting a background refresh. The child must be able to
    # acquire it, otherwise every stale report spawns a refresh that immediately skips.
    if measure_lock is not None:
        measure_lock.close()
        measure_lock = None
    fresh_paths = dropped_for_measurement | (set(known) - known_before)
    age_min = (time.time() - at) / 60 if at else 0.0
    if not a.no_snapshot:
        lock = SNAPSHOT.with_suffix(".refreshing")
        if a.refresh:
            old_at, old_sizes, old_times = load_snapshot_meta()
            merged = {**old_sizes, **known}
            save_snapshot(merged, measured_at={**old_times, **{k: time.time() for k in known}})
            lock.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        elif not at or len(known) != before or a.apply or fresh_paths:
            # Keep the global stamp old until every item has been refreshed, while recording exact item times.
            updated_times = {k: v for k, v in measured_at.items() if k in known}
            updated_times.update({k: time.time() for k in fresh_paths})
            save_snapshot(known, at=at or None, measured_at=updated_times)
        if at and age_min > a.max_age and not a.apply and not _fresh(lock, 600):
            lock.touch()
            subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "--refresh", "--top", "0",
                              "--min-mib", "0"],
                             stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                             stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, start_new_session=True)
    if at and not a.json:
        print(f"sizes from {age_min:.0f} min ago"
              f"{' (refreshing in background)' if age_min > a.max_age and not a.apply else ''};"
              " verdicts are live\n")
    shown = [fs for fs in fss.values() if not a.pressure or fs.under]
    accounting = filesystem_accounting(shown, items)
    write_health_summary(shown, items, accounting)
    # Xcode copies are sized by a walk, so only a report shows them (the agent runs with --top 0)
    tooling = (simulator_runtimes() + (xcode_items() if a.top or a.json else [])) if sweep and DARWIN else []
    if a.json:
        print(json.dumps({"free": shown[0].free, "total": shown[0].total, "idle_hours": idle,
                          "tooling": [asdict(i) for i in tooling],
                          "filesystems": [asdict(fs) for fs in shown],
                          "accounting": accounting,
                          "owners": {f.id: {"owner": f.owner, "retired": owner_retired(f)}
                                     for f in fams if f.owner},
                          "items": [asdict(i) for i in items]}, indent=2))
    else:
        report(items, shown, a.top, idle,
               {f.id: f.rebuild for f in fams if f.id == "cargo-target"}, {f.id: f for f in fams}, accounting)
        if tooling:
            print("\nXcode copies and simulator runtimes")
        for t in tooling:
            print(f"  {gib(t.bytes)}  {t.verdict:12s} {t.idle_hours:6.1f}h  {t.family} {t.path} ({', '.join(t.reasons)})")
    if a.apply:
        print()
        halt = host_busy if sweep and POLICY["defer_during_ci"] else None
        apply(items, {f.id: f for f in fams}, a.receipt,
              {fs.dev: fs.target for fs in shown} if a.pressure else None, idle, halt)
        if sweep:
            # deleting a runtime restarts CoreSimulatorService: never while a job may be using simulators
            apply_runtimes(tooling, a.receipt, host_busy)
            IDLE_STAMP.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            IDLE_STAMP.touch()
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
